/* The Project To-Do checklist seed.
 *
 * MOVED OUT OF admin.html 2026-08-14. It is ~217KB  12.8% of the page 
 * and was downloaded on every admin load and pushed down the wire again on
 * every deploy, even though the test list changes far less often than the
 * dashboard wrapped around it. admin.html now pulls it in with a dynamic
 * import, inside runProjectTestSync, so it is fetched only when the checklist
 * actually syncs rather than on every sign-in.
 *
 * Each row is [id, area, title, steps, expected, version, status, notes,
 * retestReason]. Bump the version whenever the wording changes, or the test
 * quietly goes stale  see CLAUDE.md �0 and �9.9.
 */
export const TEST_SEED = [
  [1, 'Public Site', 'Quote request form', '1. Open a new tab in your web browser and go to highlightingutah.com (this is the public website that customers see — don\'t log into anything).\n2. Near the top of the page, find and click the gold button that says \'Free Quote\'.\n3. A form will pop up. Fill in every box: your name, phone number, email, street address, city, zip code, and how you\'d like to be contacted (phone call, text, or email).\n4. Click the \'Submit\' button at the bottom of the form.\n5. Now open the admin site (the private office dashboard — ask for the login if you don\'t have it) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen.\n6. Click the words \'Quote Requests\'.', 'On the public website, you should see a thank-you message appear. In admin\'s Quote Requests list, a new card should appear at the very top with everything you just typed in it, and a small label that says \'New\'.', 1, 'Pass', '', ''],
  [2, 'Public Site', 'Quick quote widget', '1. Open a new tab in your web browser and go to highlightingutah.com (the public website that customers see — don\'t log into anything).\n2. Scroll down the entire homepage from the very top to the very bottom, looking at everything on the page as you go.\n3. Look for a short quote box somewhere on the page that only asks for a name and one way to contact you (like a phone number or email) — this is different from the full quote form with lots of boxes that you may have already tested separately.\n4. If you find a short quote box like that, fill in the name box and the contact box.\n5. Click the button to submit it.\n6. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen.\n7. Click the words \'Quote Requests\'.', 'If you found a short quote box on the homepage, it should let you fill in just a name and a way to contact you, and after you submit it, a new entry with what you typed should appear in admin\'s Quote Requests list. If there is no short quote box anywhere on the homepage, write down \'N/A\' for this test instead of a pass or fail.', 1, 'N/A', 'Could not find a quick quote widget on the site.', ''],
  [3, 'Public Site', 'Reviews / gallery / FAQ', '1. Open a new tab in your web browser and go to highlightingutah.com (the public website).\n2. Scroll down the page until you find the reviews section.\n3. Keep scrolling until you find the photo gallery section.\n4. Keep scrolling until you find the FAQ (Frequently Asked Questions) section.\n5. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen, under the heading \'Websites\'.\n6. Click the word \'Reviews\' and write down every review listed there.\n7. Click the word \'Gallery\' and write down every photo listed there.\n8. Click the words \'Hero Images\' and write down every image listed there.\n9. Click the word \'FAQ\' and write down every question and answer listed there.', 'Every review, photo, hero image, and FAQ question/answer that is listed in admin\'s Reviews, Gallery, Hero Images, and FAQ pages should also be showing on the public website\'s reviews section, photo gallery, and FAQ section. Nothing that is in admin should be missing from the public site, and nothing should be showing on the public site that isn\'t in admin.', 1, 'Pass', '', ''],
  [4, 'Public Site', 'Contact message', '1. Open a new tab in your web browser and go to highlightingutah.com (the public website).\n2. Find the contact form somewhere on the site.\n3. In the name box, type a name you will recognize later, like \'TEST MESSAGE\'.\n4. Fill in the rest of the contact form and type a message.\n5. Send the message.\n6. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen.\n7. Click the words \'Customer Messages\'.', 'Your test message should be sitting in the Inbox inside Customer Messages, showing the same name (like \'TEST MESSAGE\') and the same message text you typed and sent.', 1, 'Pass', '', ''],
  [5, 'Member Portal', 'Sign in (phone)', '1. Open a new tab in your web browser and go to highlightingutah.com (the public website).\n2. Near the top of the page, find and click \'Member Portal\' in the top menu (this is the private area where a customer checks their own account).\n3. In the phone number box, type a real customer\'s phone number.\n4. In the last name box, type that same customer\'s last name.\n5. Click the \'Sign In\' button.', 'A gold spinner should appear on the screen saying \'Opening your account\'. After a moment that spinner should disappear, and the customer\'s invoice should load along with a row of tabs across the screen.', 4, 'Pass', '', 'Sign-in errors now say what actually went wrong. If you trip the "too many attempts" limit you should be told to wait 15 minutes, not told your name is wrong.'],
  [6, 'Member Portal', 'Sign in (email)', '1. Go back to the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com that you used in the last test).\n2. If you are still signed in from before, click the button or link to sign out.\n3. This time, in the box where you\'d normally type a phone number, type that same customer\'s email address instead.\n4. In the last name box, type that customer\'s last name.\n5. Click the \'Sign In\' button.\n6. In admin, check that customer\'s record to see if there is a second email address on file for them.\n7. If there is a second email on file, sign out of the Member Portal again.\n8. Sign in one more time using that second email address along with the customer\'s last name.', 'Every time you signed in — no matter which of the customer\'s email addresses you typed — the account should open exactly the same way: loading their invoice and the row of tabs, just like when you signed in with their phone number.', 3, 'Pass', '', 'Same page change as the phone sign-in: the demo-number line is gone and failed saves no longer show a debug string.'],
  [7, 'Member Portal', 'Loading spinner', '1. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com).\n2. Type in a customer\'s phone number (or email) and last name.\n3. Click the \'Sign In\' button.\n4. Without clicking anything else, watch the screen closely.', 'The spinner should appear right away, as soon as you click Sign In. It should disappear the moment either the account finishes loading or an error message appears — it should never keep spinning forever without anything happening.', 1, 'Pass', '', ''],
  [8, 'Member Portal', 'Wrong credentials', 'AUTOMATED — npm run test:browser (test/portal.spec.js, "t8 — a wrong last name is refused, and says so") proves the mechanism on every run: a phone number with a non-matching last name gets refused and the sign-in form reappears, with nothing thrown. What is left for you is the real page: confirm the message still reads warmly (not a raw error code) and nothing looks broken on screen.\n\n1. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com).\n2. In the phone number box, type a real customer\'s phone number, but in the last name box type a last name that does NOT belong to that customer, so the two don\'t match.\n3. Click the \'Sign In\' button.', 'A friendly message should appear saying something like \'we couldn\'t find an account.\' The sign-in form should reappear so you can try again. You should NOT see a technical error code, and you should NOT see a blank screen.', 3, 'Pass', '', 'Wired to the automated regression test (test/portal.spec.js). Reworded to point at what that test can\'t check — the real page — instead of re-proving the mechanism by hand.'],
  [9, 'Member Portal', 'Personalized link', 'AUTOMATED — npm run test:browser (test/portal.spec.js, "t9 — a personalised link opens straight to the account") proves the mechanism end-to-end against a fake backend: a token in the URL loads the account and skips the sign-in form. The earlier "Fail" here traced to a bug in the TEST HARNESS, not the app — the real portalInvoice function has always returned the shape the page expects. What is left for you is confirming that against a REAL emailed link and REAL customer data, since an automated test is never allowed to touch the real backend (CLAUDE.md §9.4).\n\n1. Find an invoice email or an RSVP email that was sent to a customer (check your own email inbox from a test email that was sent to you).\n2. Inside that email, find the personalized link to the Member Portal.\n3. Copy that link.\n4. Open a new browser tab or window where you are NOT already signed in to the Member Portal.\n5. Paste the link into the address bar and go to it.', 'The link should open straight into that customer\'s account, showing the spinner while it loads. It should NOT ask you to sign in again — no phone number or last name box should appear.', 3, 'Fail', '', 'Wired to the automated regression test (test/portal.spec.js). The failure traced to a test-harness bug, not the app — reworded to ask you to confirm against a real emailed link rather than re-prove the mechanism by hand.  Also: a personalised link now opens the portal even for a customer who has NO invoice yet — that used to say "we couldn\u2019t find an account".'],
  [10, 'Member Portal', 'Balance reflects credits', 'AUTOMATED - the credit maths is proved on every commit by run-all.js (credits reduce what is owed, a credit larger than the bill still reads Paid in Full, one exactly equal to the bill reads Paid in Full, and a balance never goes negative). What is left for you is the VISIBLE half: sign into the portal as a customer with a credit and confirm the balance on screen matches, and that the credit is explained rather than the number just being mysteriously smaller.\n\n1. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word \'Invoices\'.\n2. Click \'Invoice List\'.\n3. Find a customer and add a credit to their invoice — or, if you\'d rather not change anything, pick a customer who already has a credit on their invoice.\n4. Write down four numbers from that customer\'s invoice: the install amount, the removal amount, the credit amount, and the amount they have already paid.\n5. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com) and sign in as that same customer.\n6. Click the \'Payment\' tab.\n7. Look at the balance shown there.', 'The balance shown on the Payment tab should equal: the install amount plus the removal amount, minus the credit, minus whatever the customer has already paid. If you add up install and removal, subtract the credit, and subtract what\'s already been paid, that final number should match the balance shown in the portal exactly.', 4, 'N/A', '', 'The credit maths is now automated. Reworded to check only what the customer sees in the portal.  Also: the balance is now itemised — installation, removal, each light-change fee and each credit with its reason, then the total. Check the lines add up to the number shown.'],
  [11, 'Member Portal', 'Pay with PayPal', 'AUTOMATED (the decision to show the button) — npm run test:browser (test/portal.spec.js, "t11 — a PayPal button renders when the provider allows cards") proves the portal renders a PayPal button whenever Site Settings say to (Payment Integrations set to PayPal or Both, with a Client ID saved) — against a fake PayPal SDK, since a browser test can never be allowed to move real money (CLAUDE.md §9.11). If you still only see Venmo after checking that setting, the code is proven working — the setting is the first thing to check, not a bug to chase. What is left for you is the real thing automation can never do: an actual payment.\n\n1. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com) and sign in as a customer who still owes money — use your own test customer record for this, not a real customer.\n2. Stay on the \'Payment\' tab.\n3. Look for a PayPal button on that tab. Which buttons appear is controlled in admin under Websites > Site Settings > Payment Integrations: \'Venmo only\' shows just the Venmo button, \'PayPal only\' shows just PayPal, and \'Both\' shows Venmo and PayPal together. PayPal also needs a Client ID saved there before it will appear at all. Check that setting first — if it is set to \'Venmo only\', seeing only Venmo is correct and is not a fail.\n4. If there is an option to add a small tip, add one.\n5. Click through and complete a real payment for a small amount using PayPal.\n6. After the payment finishes, go back to the Member Portal and reload the page.', 'PayPal should charge you the exact balance that was shown on the Payment tab, plus whatever tip you added. After the payment goes through and you reload the portal, the balance should have dropped, and the account should now say \'Paid in Full\'.', 4, 'Fail', 'There is only a link to Venmo.', 'Wired to the automated regression test (test/portal.spec.js), which now proves the button-rendering decision. Reworded to rule out the code first and point straight at the Site Settings check + the real payment, so this isn\'t re-proving what\'s already covered.  Also: the payment area now shows when we last received a payment, and the whole card is hidden for a customer who has no bill yet.'],
  [12, 'Member Portal', 'Change light colours', '1. Pick a test customer to use for this test — do NOT use a real customer, because changing light colours through the portal charges the customer a $30 fee.\n2. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com) and sign in as that test customer.\n3. Click the \'Light Colors\' tab (if you\'re using a phone, this tab is in the row of tabs across the bottom of the screen).\n4. Change the light colours to something different than what\'s currently selected.\n5. Click \'Save\'.\n6. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word \'Customers\'.\n7. Click \'All Customers\'.\n8. Find that test customer in the list and click \'Edit\' on their entry.\n9. Go back to the menu on the left side of the screen and click the word \'Warehouse\'.\n10. Click the \'Build\' tab.', 'The portal should show a message confirming the colour change was saved. In admin, the Edit Customer popup for that person should show the new colours you picked. That same customer should now also appear in the Warehouse Build list.', 2, 'Pass', '', 'IMPORTANT \u2014 this one was CHARGING PEOPLE WRONGLY. Opening Light Colors and pressing Save without changing anything used to add a $30 fee and delete the pattern note. It should now say "Nothing changed \u2014 no charge". Also check a real change KEEPS any note in brackets, e.g. "(every third bulb)", and that the colour swatches are big enough not to mis-tap.'],
  [13, 'Member Portal', 'Change contact info', '1. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com) and sign in as a test customer.\n2. Click the \'Information\' tab.\n3. Change the phone number to a different test value.\n4. Change the gate code to a different test value.\n5. Change the street address to a different test value.\n6. Click \'Save\'.\n7. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word \'Customers\'.\n8. Click \'All Customers\'.\n9. Find that test customer and click \'Edit\' on their entry.\n10. Go back to the menu on the left side of the screen and click the word \'Routes\'.\n11. Find an upcoming saved route that includes that test customer as a stop.', 'The Edit Customer popup should show the new phone number, gate code, and street address you just typed in the portal. The upcoming route stop for that customer should also show the new gate code and the new address.', 1, 'Pass', '', ''],
  [14, 'Member Portal', 'Change preferences', 'AUTOMATED (customer side only) — npm run test:browser (test/portal.spec.js, "t14 — changing a preference sends it to the server") proves a changed preference on the Information tab reaches the server as a portalSave call. That is only the sign-out-and-send half — it is NOT what this checklist test is actually about. The ADMIN SIDE, whether those fields show up anywhere on the Edit Customer popup, has no automated coverage at all and is exactly what this manual pass should focus on.\n\n1. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com) and sign in as a test customer.\n2. Click the \'Information\' tab and look through it, then also click the \'Changes\' tab and look through it.\n3. Change the wire colour to a different value.\n4. Save that change.\n5. Change the answer to the outlet timer question.\n6. Save that change.\n7. Change the install timing preference.\n8. Save that change.\n9. Type something new into the notes box.\n10. Save that change.\n11. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word \'Customers\'.\n12. Click \'All Customers\'.\n13. Find that test customer and click \'Edit\' on their entry.\n14. On the Edit Customer popup, look for each of the four things you just changed: the wire colour, the outlet timer answer, the install timing preference, and the notes.', 'All four things you changed — the wire colour, the outlet timer answer, the install timing preference, and the notes — should be visible somewhere on the Edit Customer popup in admin. If even one of those four is nowhere to be found on that popup, this test fails.', 2, 'Fail', 'There is nowhere to view it on the admin page.', 'Wired to the automated regression test (test/portal.spec.js), which covers only the customer-side send — the admin-visibility gap this test is actually about is still untouched. Reworded so the manual pass goes straight there instead of re-proving the Save button works.'],
  [15, 'Member Portal', 'RSVP yes / no / back next year', 'AUTOMATED (one slice) — npm run test:browser (test/portal.spec.js, "t15 — an RSVP link records the answer without asking them to sign in") proves an RSVP=yes link records the answer without a sign-in prompt. That is only the sign-in-free part of Run one below. The recycling behaviour in admin, the \'no\' and \'back next year\' paths, and the messages shown after each answer all still need this full manual pass.\n\n1. Pick three different test customers, one for each of the three runs below. Do not use real customers, since these are RSVP tests.\n2. Run one: Sign in to the Member Portal as the first test customer and choose \'RSVP Yes\'.\n3. When asked whether they want any changes, answer \'no\'.\n4. Notice where the portal takes you after you answer \'no\'.\n5. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard), click \'Customers\' in the menu on the left side of the screen, then click \'All Customers\', then click \'Edit\' on that first test customer.\n6. Still in admin, click \'Warehouse\' in the menu on the left side of the screen, then click the \'Recycle\' tab, and check whether that first test customer is listed there.\n7. Run two: Sign in to the Member Portal as the second test customer and choose \'RSVP Back Next Year\'.\n8. Open admin, click \'Customers\', then \'All Customers\', then \'Edit\' on that second test customer.\n9. Still in admin, click \'Warehouse\', then the \'Recycle\' tab, and check whether that second test customer is listed there.\n10. Run three: Sign in to the Member Portal as the third test customer and choose \'RSVP No\'.\n11. Type a reason you\'ll recognize into the reason box.\n12. Submit it.\n13. Open admin, click \'Customers\', then \'All Customers\', then \'Edit\' on that third test customer.\n14. Still in admin, click \'Warehouse\', then the \'Recycle\' tab, and check whether that third test customer is listed there.', 'Run one (RSVP Yes) should end with a thank-you message, and that customer should NOT show up in the Warehouse Recycle list. Run two (RSVP Back Next Year) should show a message saying something like \'we look forward to seeing you next year,\' and that customer should also NOT show up in the Warehouse Recycle list. Run three (RSVP No) is the only one where the customer should show up in the Warehouse Recycle list.', 3, 'Fail', '', 'Wired to the automated regression test (test/portal.spec.js), which covers only the no-sign-in-required slice of Run one. Reworded so the manual pass doesn\'t re-prove that slice — the recycling and message checks across all three runs are still fully manual.  Also: RSVP is now available inside the portal on the Changes tab, not only from the emailed link. Check all three buttons, and that "Not this season" asks before saving.'],
  [16, 'Member Portal', 'Request cancellation', '1. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com) and sign in as a test customer.\n2. Click the \'Cancel\' tab.\n3. In the reason box, type a reason you\'ll recognize later.\n4. Submit the cancellation.\n5. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word \'Customers\'.\n6. Click \'All Customers\'.\n7. Find that test customer\'s card in the list.', 'That test customer\'s card in admin should show the words \'Cancellation Requested\', and the reason you typed should be readable somewhere on that card.', 1, 'Pass', '', ''],
  [17, 'Member Portal', 'Quote review', 'AUTOMATED (the sign-in and the quote-review screen) — npm run test:browser (test/portal.spec.js, "t17 — a customer with a pending quote is offered approve or decline") proves that signing in with just a phone + last name for someone who has a priced quote but no invoice yet shows the quote details, the price, and both an Approve and a Decline button — against a fake backend. What is left for you is clicking through: does Approve/Decline actually go through and update things.\n\n1. Open the admin site (the private office dashboard) and look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the words \'Quote Requests\'.\n2. Find someone in that list who has already been given a price (has been quoted) but is not yet a paying customer (they have no invoice).\n3. Write down the phone number and last name listed on that quote.\n4. Open the Member Portal (the customer login area on highlightingutah.com).\n5. Type in the phone number and last name you wrote down.\n6. Click \'Sign In\'.\n7. Click \'Approve My Quote\' (or \'Not Right Now\' to decline) and confirm it goes through.', 'Instead of showing an invoice like it would for a regular customer, the portal should show that person\'s quote details and the price they were quoted, along with an \'Approve\' button and a \'Decline\' button. Clicking through should work without errors and reflect the outcome (e.g. the quote moving to Awaiting Response / Closed in admin\'s Quote Requests).', 5, 'Fail', '', 'A REAL bug was found and fixed here, not just a test-harness one: tryShowQuoteReview() in index.html was checking the typed last name against the FIRST word of the quote\'s stored name, not a real last-name match — so typing your actual last name (as step 3 says to write down and step 5 says to type) could get refused unless the name happened to be stored surname-first. Fixed to a whole-word, word-order-independent match, same rule as everywhere else in the app. Retest with a real quote and a real typed last name.  Also: a quote link now opens the quote review ONLY \u2014 it can no longer hand back a customer account. And an existing customer with a quote waiting is now offered it underneath their balance.'],
  [18, 'Admin', 'Quotes - view & filter', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard — not highlightingutah.com, which is the public site customers see).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the words "Quote Requests".\n3. Pick any one quote card in the list and read it from the top all the way to the bottom.\n4. Near the top of the page, find the filter control.\n5. Use the filter to switch it to "New only".\n6. Use the filter to switch it to show all quotes.', 'Each quote card should show the customer\'s name, its status, their address, their contact details, and controls for adding or managing a photo. When you switch the filter to \'New only\', the list should get shorter, showing fewer cards. When you switch it back to show all quotes, the list should get longer again.', 1, 'Pass', '', ''],
  [19, 'Admin', 'Quotes - price a quote', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Quote Requests".\n3. Find a quote card marked "New" that is one of your own test submissions (not a real customer\'s).\n4. On that card, find the box called "Estimated Feet" and type a number into it.\n5. Find the box called "Quoted Price" and type a price into it.\n6. Click anywhere outside that box (or press the Tab key). The price saves by itself the moment you click out, and a small message pops up confirming it - there is no Save button to press.\n7. Find and click the button called "Send quote email" - this shows you a link plus a filled-in email right below the button.\n8. The card now sits under the "Awaiting Response" tab. Find it there and open its "Pricing & Approval" panel again.\n9. Type a different, higher price into the "Quoted Price" box - as if the customer had just called and asked you to add another part of the house - then click outside the box.\n10. Refresh the whole page in your browser, then open that card\'s "Pricing & Approval" panel one more time and read the price.\n11. Copy the link shown in that box and open it in your browser, as if you were the customer.\n12. On that page, find and click the button the customer would press to accept the quote.', 'The feet and price you typed should save on the card on their own, without pressing any Save button. Clicking "Send quote email" should show a link and an editable email underneath it, and the card should move to "Awaiting Response". The higher price you typed in step 9 must still be there after the refresh in step 10 - it must NOT snap back to the old price - and the customer\'s page at that link should show that new higher price too. When you click the accept button on that page, it should work without any errors.', 3, 'Retest', 'The approval link works but the accept-my-quote button does not.', 'The Quoted Price box now saves on its own when you click out of it. It used to save ONLY if you went on to press the send button, so changing the price on a quote that had already gone out silently reverted. Steps 8-10 are new and test exactly that. The button name is also corrected to "Send quote email", which is what it actually says on screen.'],
  [20, 'Admin', 'Quotes - detail form', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Quote Requests".\n3. Approve a test quote (one of your own test submissions).\n4. Open the link shown under the "Send Email" button for that quote in your browser, as if you were the customer.\n5. On the public site, fill in the customer\'s own detail form: choose a wire colour, choose an outlet timer, and choose an install month.\n6. Submit that form.\n7. Go back to admin and open "Quote Requests" again.\n8. Near the top, find and click the tab called "Form".\n9. Find the same quote you just submitted the form for.\n10. Now find a different approved quote where the customer has NOT yet submitted their detail form.', 'For the quote where the customer submitted the form, the card should show the Wire colour and Timer choices they picked, right on the card itself. For the quote where the customer has NOT submitted the form yet, those lines should simply not appear. There is no separate \'Detail Form\' section to open or edit in admin anymore, because customers now fill that form in on their own portal after their quote is approved.', 2, 'Retest', 'Probably should not ask which outlet - the crew decides that - but do ask if they have one.', 'The admin-side Detail Form was removed from the quote card in the P1 rework - it\'s now filled in on the customer\'s own portal after they approve, not edited here. This Pass needs a fresh look against how it actually works now.'],
  [21, 'Admin', 'Quotes - convert to customer (manual)', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Quote Requests".\n3. Find an approved quote where the customer has already submitted their detail form back to you.\n4. Click the button called "Convert to Customer". A popup should appear asking HOW you want to convert them - it did not used to do this, it used to go straight to the form.\n5. Read the popup. Then click the button that says "Fill in manually".\n6. Check that all of the following carried across correctly into the form: the light colours, the house areas, the outlet timer, the specific outlet, the install timing, any notes, the photo (with its markup/drawing on it), the approved price, the contact preference, and the "Charge $30 one-time set-up fee" tick box.\n7. Type a number into the "Measured Feet" box, and while you type, watch both the "Number of Bins" box and the line underneath the Customer # box.\n8. Click the button called "Add Customer".\n9. Go back to "Quote Requests" in the left menu.', 'The popup should offer three things: "Convert automatically", "Fill in manually", and Cancel. Clicking "Fill in manually" should open the Add a Customer form already filled in - the same thing the button used to do on its own. Every detail listed in step 6 should have carried across, including the $30 set-up fee tick matching what was ticked on the quote. In step 7, the Number of Bins box and the line under Customer # should both update as you type, and they should agree with each other. A new customer record should exist with every one of those details on it, at the price the customer approved. Back in Quote Requests, that quote should now say Converted.', 2, 'Pass', '', 'Convert to Customer now asks automatic-or-manual first. This test covers the manual half; test 205 covers the automatic half. Also added: the $30 fee and the live Number of Bins box.'],
  [22, 'Admin', 'Add Customer - full detail', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Customers".\n3. Click the tab called "Add a Customer".\n4. Before filling anything else in, type just a street address and a city, then click the button called "Add Customer". Read the red "Still missing" box that appears directly above that button, and read the grey message next to the Add Customer button. Note that NO popup appears this time.\n5. Now tick one light colour, and nothing else. Click "Add Customer" again. This time a popup should appear listing what is still missing. Click Cancel on it.\n6. Now fill in every single box on the form: name, phone, email, address, light colours, house parts, install timing, house notes, outlet timer, specific outlet, gate code, measured feet, and price.\n7. Upload a house photo.\n8. Highlight (mark up and draw on) that photo.\n9. Click the button called "Add Customer".\n10. Click the tab called "All Customers".\n11. Find the test customer you just added.\n12. Click "Edit" on their record.', 'At step 4 the red "Still missing" box should name all FIVE things left blank at once, rather than one at a time - Phone Number, Email, Light Colors, House Picture and Total Price. No popup should appear this time, and the grey message beside the button should tell you to pick their light colours, because a customer with no colours never reaches the Warehouse to be built. Nothing should be saved.\n\nLight colours are the one thing on this form you cannot skip. Everything else can deliberately be filled in later - the photo gets taken next week, the price is still being agreed - so at step 5, once a colour is ticked, you should finally get the popup, listing the four that are left and offering to add them anyway. Pressing Cancel should leave the form exactly as you left it with nothing saved.\n\nAt the end, a popup window should open showing the customer\'s information, and every single thing you typed or uploaded in steps 6 through 8 should be there. Nothing should be missing or quietly dropped.', 4,'Pass', '', 'Add Customer now lists everything that is missing in one go, in a red box above the button, and can still be overridden on purpose. A house Google cannot place on the map is now saved anyway with a warning, instead of being refused.\n\nUpdated 2026-08-15: LIGHT COLOURS ARE NOW REQUIRED and are the one thing on this form that CANNOT be overridden - the old "no colours picked, add them anyway?" prompt is gone. That is what steps 4 and 5 check now, and it is why the step numbers after them shifted by one. The reason: a customer saved with no colours is invisible to the Warehouse, so they were never built for and nothing said so.'],
  [23, 'Admin', 'Add Customer - feet drives number preview', 'AUTOMATED - the feet-to-bins rule is checked on every commit against the real cnBinsForFeet from js/money.js, at the true 260ft cutoff. You no longer need to prove the rule. What is left is the screen: that the Add a Customer form PREVIEWS the right bin count and number series as you type the feet in.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Customers".\n3. Click the tab called "Add a Customer".\n4. Find the box called "Measured Feet" and type the number 150 into it.\n5. Look at the preview near that box for the bin count and the customer number it\'s about to assign. Also look at the box called "Number of Bins". Write down what both of them say.\n6. Without saving anything, clear that box and type the number 261 into it instead.\n7. Look at the same preview and the same Number of Bins box again, and write down what they say now.\n8. Clear the box one more time and type 600 into it.\n9. Write down what the preview and the Number of Bins box say a third time.', 'At 150 feet: 1 bin and a normal (regular) customer number. At 261 feet: 2 bins and a customer number starting with 5, like 5001 (a \'5000-series\' number). At 600 feet: 3 bins, still a 5000-series number. Every time, the "Number of Bins" box should show the SAME number as the preview - they should never disagree - and both should update immediately as you type, without you having to save or click anything else.', 4, 'Retest', '', 'The Number of Bins box now follows the feet instead of sitting on 1, so this checks the box and the preview agree. Added a 600 ft step because bins now go up in 260s instead of stopping at 2.'],
  [24, 'Admin', 'Add Customer - photo markup', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Customers".\n3. Click the tab called "Add a Customer".\n4. Upload a house photo.\n5. Click the button called "Mark Up" (the tool for drawing on the photo).\n6. Using the red drawing tool, draw a red line on the photo.\n7. Using the yellow pen tool, draw something else on the photo.\n8. Click the button to save the markup.\n9. Finish filling in the form and click "Add Customer".\n10. Click the tab called "All Customers".\n11. Find the test customer you just added and click "Edit" on their record.', 'The photo should still be there, and it should still show both your red line and your yellow pen mark on it. The markup should have saved along with the record.', 1, 'Pass', '', ''],
  [25, 'Admin', 'Edit Customer - edit every field', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Customers".\n3. Click the tab called "All Customers".\n4. Find a test customer and click "Edit" on their record.\n5. Change a little bit of every one of these fields: name, phone, email, address, light colours, house areas, install timing, gate code, outlet, notes, measured feet, price, bins, wire colour, and RSVP (whether they said yes or no to having lights this year).\n6. Click the button called "Save".\n7. Reopen the same "Edit" popup for that same customer.\n8. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Warehouse".\n9. Click the tab called "Build".', 'Every single change you made in step 5 should still be there when you reopen the Edit popup, and nothing else on the record should have been erased or reset. Also, because you changed the light colours, that same customer should now appear in the Warehouse Build list.', 1, 'Pass', '', ''],
  [26, 'Admin', 'Edit Customer - photo + markup here', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Customers".\n3. Click the tab called "All Customers".\n4. Find a customer who already has a house photo on their record, and click "Edit" on them.\n5. Replace their photo with a different photo.\n6. Click the button called "Highlight" or "Mark Up Photo".\n7. Draw on the new photo.\n8. Click the button called "Save".\n9. Reopen the same "Edit" popup for that same customer.', 'The new photo you uploaded, along with the markup you drew, should both be saved and visible when you reopen the Edit popup. Also keep in mind that this Edit popup should be the only place in the app where photos can be edited — you should not be able to change or mark up a photo from the route card or from the invoice.', 1, 'Pass', '', ''],
  [27, 'Admin', 'Edit Customer - house price to invoice', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a customer who pays only for their own house, not someone who is billed for and covers several houses at once.\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Invoices".\n4. Click "Invoice List".\n5. Find that customer and write down their current invoice amount.\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Customers".\n7. Click the tab called "All Customers".\n8. Find the same customer and click "Edit" on their record.\n9. Change their house price.\n10. Click "Save".\n11. Go back to "Invoices", then "Invoice List".\n12. Find that same customer again.', 'The invoice amount should now match the new price you set, not the old one. If that customer already had the $30 new-member fee added to their invoice before you made this change, that $30 fee should still be included on the invoice afterward.', 2, 'Pass', '', 'Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path.'],
  [28, 'Admin', 'Edit Customer - payer group guard', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Find a payer who covers several houses. In admin, this means more than one house is billed to the same phone number.\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Invoices".\n4. Click "Invoice List".\n5. Find that payer and write down their invoice total, and write down which houses that invoice covers.\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Customers".\n7. Click the tab called "All Customers".\n8. Click "Edit" on just one of those houses, not all of them.\n9. Change that one house\'s price.\n10. Click "Save".\n11. Go back to "Invoices", then "Invoice List".\n12. Find that same payer again.', 'The invoice total should be recalculated using all of that payer\'s houses added together, not just the one you changed. Every house that was on the invoice before should still be listed on it — none of the other houses should have disappeared.', 3, 'N/A', '', 'Payer-group behaviour changed — a multi-house invoice now has editable per-property prices and a read-only total.'],
  [29, 'Admin', 'Edit Customer - feet crosses 260', 'AUTOMATED - the 260ft boundary itself is covered on every commit, and run-all.js also checks that changing feet warns before renumbering a labelled bin. What is left for a person is the warning actually appearing on screen, and reading clearly enough that nobody clicks through it by accident.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Customers".\n3. Click the tab called "All Customers".\n4. Find a customer who already has a customer number assigned.\n5. Click "Edit" on their record.\n6. Change their "Measured Feet" number so that it crosses the 260-foot line — for example, change it from 240 up to 280, or from 270 down to 150.\n7. Click the button called "Save".', 'Before saving the change, the app should warn you that this will change the customer\'s bin count and their number series (the range their customer number comes from). It should not silently renumber them without telling you first.', 3, 'Retest', '', 'The boundary and the warning\'s existence are automated. Reworded to check the warning appears and reads clearly.'],
  [30, 'Admin', 'Quote declined', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Quote Requests".\n3. Pick a test quote, price it, and click "Send Email" to generate its link.\n4. Open that link in your browser, as if you were the customer.\n5. On that page, click "Decline" instead of "Approve".\n6. Go back to admin and open "Quote Requests" again.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Customers".\n8. Click the tab called "All Customers".', 'The quote card in Quote Requests should now show that it was declined. In All Customers, no new customer record should have been created from that declined quote.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [31, 'Admin', 'Quote approve/decline lands in Inbox', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Quote Requests".\n3. Pick a test quote, price it, and click "Send Email" to generate its link.\n4. Open that link in your browser, as if you were the customer.\n5. On that page, click "Approve".\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Customer Messages".\n7. Look inside the main Inbox for a notice about this approval.\n8. Also look inside the pinned "System" folder for the same notice.\n9. Now repeat all of the steps above, but this time pick a different test quote and click "Decline" instead of "Approve" on its link.\n10. Check the main Inbox and the pinned "System" folder again for this decline notice.', 'Both the approval notice and the decline notice should show up in the main Inbox, not in the pinned System folder, because both of them need you to make a decision — they shouldn\'t be filed away automatically.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [32, 'Admin', 'Estimated feet autosaves', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Quote Requests".\n3. Pick any quote card.\n4. Find the box called "Estimated Feet" on that card and type a number into it.\n5. Do NOT click any save button.\n6. Click on an empty area somewhere else on the page, not the box you just typed in.\n7. Refresh the whole browser page (reload it).\n8. Find that same quote card again.', 'The number you typed into Estimated Feet should still be there after the page refreshes. It should have saved itself automatically when you clicked away from the box, even though you never pressed a save button.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [33, 'Admin', 'Quote token is unique', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Quote Requests".\n3. Pick a test quote, price it, and click "Send Email" to generate its link.\n4. Pick a second, different test quote and click "Send Email" on it too.\n5. Copy both links somewhere you can compare them side by side, like a notes app.\n6. Open the first link in your browser.\n7. Open the second link in your browser.', 'The two links should be different from each other. Each link should only ever show the details for its own quote — the first link should never show you the second customer\'s details, and vice versa.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [34, 'Customer Numbers', '260 ft rule', 'AUTOMATED - the 260ft rule itself is now checked on every commit by run-all.js, which calls the real cnBinsForFeet out of js/money.js (this used to be tested against a fake copy inside the test file that said 200ft, so it could never fail). You no longer need to prove the rule; you only need to confirm the SCREEN shows the right bin count and number series.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard - the website you log into, not highlightingutah.com, which is the public site customers see).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word "Customers".\n3. Click the button that says "Add a Customer".\n4. A form opens. In the box called "Measured Feet", type the number 259. This is a test/fake customer, don\'t worry about filling in anything else yet.\n5. Look at what the form shows for the number of bins and the customer number it\'s about to offer, and write both down.\n6. Without leaving the form, change the Measured Feet box to 260 instead.\n7. Write down the bins and number it shows now.\n8. Change the Measured Feet box one more time to 261.\n9. Write down the bins and number a third time.\n10. Now change it to 521.\n11. Write down the bins and number a fourth time.\n12. Compare all four sets of notes.', 'At 259 feet and at 260 feet, it should show 1 bin and offer a normal (regular) customer number. At 261 feet, it should show 2 bins and offer a customer number starting with the digit 5 (a "5000-series" number, like 5001), instead of a normal number. At 521 feet it should show 3 bins - a house needs another bin for every 260 feet - and still offer a 5000-series number, because there are only two kinds of number and anything past one bin uses the 5000 kind.', 3, '', '', 'The 260 ft cutoff has NOT moved - 260 is still 1 bin and 261 is still 2. What is new is that bins no longer stop at 2: a house needs another bin every 260 feet, so 521 ft is 3 bins. Added a 521 ft step to check that.'],
  [35, 'Customer Numbers', 'No feet, no number', 'AUTOMATED - run-all.js checks that blank or junk feet never becomes a two-bin house. What is left for a person is the screen behaviour: that the app refuses to hand out a number and says so clearly.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers".\n3. Click the button that says "Add a Customer".\n4. Fill in the rest of the test customer\'s information, but leave the box called "Measured Feet" completely blank.\n5. Save the test customer.\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the words "Customer Numbers".\n7. Click the tab called "Assign".\n8. Try to give that test customer a customer number.', 'The system should refuse to hand out a customer number and should show a message explaining why (because Measured Feet was left blank), instead of guessing a bin count or handing out a random number anyway.', 2, '', '', 'The logic is now covered automatically. Reworded to cover only the on-screen refusal.'],
  [36, 'Customer Numbers', 'Pool is used first', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the words "Customer Numbers".\n3. Click the tab called "Available Pool".\n4. Look at the list of numbers shown and write down the lowest (smallest) number in it.\n5. Note whether that lowest number is a normal number or a "5000-series" number (one that starts with the digit 5, like 5001).\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers".\n7. Click the button that says "Add a Customer".\n8. Add a new test customer, entering a Measured Feet value that would put them in that same series you noted in step 5 (over 260 feet if the lowest number was a 5000-series number, or under 260 feet if it was a normal number).\n9. Save the test customer.\n10. Give this new customer a customer number.', 'The new customer should be given the exact lowest number you wrote down from the Available Pool in step 4 - not a brand-new number that has never been used before.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [37, 'Customer Numbers', 'Assigning removes from pool', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the words "Customer Numbers".\n3. Click the tab called "Available Pool".\n4. Write down any one number you see sitting in that list.\n5. Assign that exact number to a customer.\n6. Click the tab called "Available Pool" again to go back to it.\n7. Refresh the page in your web browser.', 'The number you just assigned should no longer appear anywhere in the Available Pool list, so it can\'t be handed out to a second person by mistake.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [38, 'Customer Numbers', 'Empty pool takes the next number up', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the words "Customer Numbers".\n3. Click the tab called "Available Pool".\n4. Look through the list and find a series (either the normal numbers or the 5000-series numbers) that has nothing left sitting in the pool - completely empty.\n5. Click the tab called "All Numbers".\n6. Look through that same series and write down the highest number currently in use.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers".\n8. Click the button that says "Add a Customer".\n9. Add a test customer whose Measured Feet puts them in that same empty series.\n10. Save the test customer.\n11. Assign that customer a customer number.', 'The new customer should be given the number exactly one higher than the highest number you wrote down in step 6 - for example, if the highest in-use number in that series was 5010, they should get 5011.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [39, 'Customer Numbers', 'Bulk assign', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the words "Customer Numbers".\n3. Click the tab called "Assign".\n4. Click the button that says "Check First" (this is a dry run - a preview that does not actually change anything yet).\n5. Read what the preview says it would do.\n6. Run the bulk assign process on several customers who do not yet have a customer number.\n7. Click the tab called "Available Pool".\n8. Look at the list of numbers left in the pool.', 'Every customer whose house needs 2 bins should have been given a 5000-series number (starting with the digit 5), and every customer whose house needs only 1 bin should have been given a normal number. Afterward, the Available Pool list should have shrunk by exactly the number of customer numbers that were just handed out - no more, no less.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [40, 'Customer Numbers', 'Duplicate number report', 'AUTOMATED - run-all.js actually RUNS the Health Check engine against made-up data and confirms it catches a duplicate customer number, leaves a unique one alone, and deliberately offers NO auto-fix (it cannot know which house keeps the number). What is left is the report on screen: that it lists the right houses and that the no-auto-fix wording is clear.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers".\n3. Click "All Customers".\n4. Click Edit on a test customer\'s record and give them a customer number. Write that number down.\n5. Save that customer.\n6. Click Edit on a second test customer\'s record and give them that exact same customer number on purpose.\n7. Save that second customer.\n8. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the words "Health Check".\n9. Click the button that says "Run Health Check".\n10. Look through the results for the section about duplicate customer numbers.', 'The duplicate customer number section should list both test customers by name as sharing the same number. There should be no automatic "fix" button next to this finding - the system can\'t know which customer should keep the number, so a person has to make that decision.', 2, '', '', 'The duplicate detection is now executed by an automated test. Reworded to check the on-screen report.'],
  [41, 'Warehouse', 'Build queue membership', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers".\n3. Click "All Customers".\n4. Find a customer who already has light colours set and click Edit on their record.\n5. Change one of their light colours to a different colour.\n6. Click Save. (This is what puts them in the warehouse build queue.)\n7. Click Edit on a second customer\'s record.\n8. Clear out that second customer\'s light colours completely, so none are set.\n9. Click Save.\n10. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse".\n11. Click the tab called "Build".\n12. Find the colour group the first customer belongs to and press the button on the right of that row that says "Show 1 house" (the number depends on how many are in the group).', 'The group should open when you press that button, and the first customer - the one whose colour you changed - should be listed by name inside it. The button should change to say "Hide". The second customer, whose colours you cleared, should not appear anywhere in the build list, even though you just edited and saved their record too.', 2, '', '', 'The colour groups now have a real "Show houses" button instead of only a small arrow, because the names inside were impossible to find. Please check the button opens the group and the name is listed.'],
  [42, 'Warehouse', 'Bundle count', 'AUTOMATED - bundle maths is ceil(feet / 40) and run-all.js proves it on every commit, including that 40ft is one bundle, 41ft rounds up to two, and that the bundle size is still 40. What is left is the warehouse screen showing that number in the right place.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers".\n3. Click "All Customers".\n4. Find a customer who is already sitting in the warehouse build queue and click Edit on their record.\n5. In the box called "Measured Feet", type the number 100 exactly.\n6. Click Save.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse".\n8. Click the tab called "Build".\n9. Find that same customer in the list.', 'That customer\'s bundle count should read 3. (This is 100 feet divided by 40 feet per bundle, which equals 2.5, rounded up to the next whole number, 3.)', 2, '', '', 'Bundle maths is automated. Reworded to check the number shown on the warehouse queue.'],
  [43, 'Warehouse', 'Colour grouping', 'AUTOMATED (and a real bug was fixed here on 2026-08-14) - the warehouse used to put the SAME pattern into two separate groups if the colours had been typed in a different order, so \'Red, Green\' and \'Green, Red\' built as two groups and the same bundle got made twice. whGroupKey now sorts the colours first, in BOTH admin and the crew portal, and run-all.js checks that on every commit - including that repeated colours still make a different pattern (\'Red, Red, Green\' is not \'Red, Green, Green\'), that wire colour still separates groups, and that admin and the crew portal have not drifted apart. What is left for a person is the screen: look at the build queue and confirm the groups are right and the counts add up. If you have two groups that should have been one, they should now be merged.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers".\n3. Click "All Customers".\n4. Click Edit on a test customer\'s record.\n5. Set their light colours to a specific pattern (for example, all red).\n6. Click Save. (This puts them in the warehouse build queue.)\n7. Repeat steps 4 through 6 for two or three more test customers, giving each one that exact same colour pattern, until you have three or four test customers all sharing the identical pattern.\n8. Click Edit on one more test customer and give them a different colour pattern instead.\n9. Click Save.\n10. Now the part that is new: click Edit on ONE more test customer, and instead of ticking the colour boxes, type the SAME colours as the first group as plain words - for example "red and green" in lower case, or "Red/Green". Save.\n11. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse".\n12. Click the tab called "Build".\n13. Open the crew portal (employee.html) and look at its Lights tab too, so you can compare.', 'The three or four customers who share the identical colour pattern should be grouped together under that one pattern, with their bundle counts added together into a single total. The customer with the different pattern should sit by itself in its own separate group.\n\nThe customer from step 10 - the one typed as words instead of ticked - should be in the SAME group as the others, not in a group of their own. That is what changed: the warehouse now reads "red and green", "Red & Green", "Red/Green" and "red green" as the same build as "Red, Green".\n\nThe crew portal should show exactly the same groups as admin. If the two disagree, that is a fail.', 3, '', '', 'The grouping bug is fixed and the rule is now automated. Worth re-running because the groups on screen will have MERGED - confirm the new counts are right. Updated again 2026-08-15: the warehouse now also reads colours typed as WORDS ("red and green", "Red/Green", "red green") as the same build as the ticked "Red, Green", so more groups will have merged. Step 10 and the crew-portal comparison are new.'],
  [44, 'Warehouse', 'Mark built', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse".\n3. Click the tab called "Build".\n4. Press the "Show houses" button on any colour group to open it.\n5. Pick any one house listed inside and press the "Edit" button next to their name.\n6. On the popup that opens, change something the warehouse cares about - the wire colour, the measured feet, or the light colours - and press Save.\n7. Look at that house’s row in the Build list again.\n8. If a "Timers" group is showing at the top of the Build tab, press its "Show houses" button and press "Edit" on a house in there too, then close the popup without saving.\n9. Now press "Mark Done" next to a house in a colour group.\n10. Refresh the page in your web browser.\n11. Click the tab called "Build" again, and press "Show houses" on that same group.', 'Step 5 should open the normal Edit Customer popup for that house, without leaving the Warehouse tab. After saving in step 6, the Build list should show the change straight away - a new wire colour on the row, a different bundle count for new feet, or the house moving into a different colour group. The Timers list should have an Edit button too. The house you marked done in step 9 should no longer appear anywhere in the build list, and should stay gone after refreshing - it should not come back. The Timers group should still have NO Mark Done of its own: marking the house done in its colour group is what finishes it.', 4, '', '', 'Every house row in Warehouse now has an Edit button - in the colour groups, in the Timers roll-up, and on the Recycle tab - because everything shown on those rows (colours, wire, feet, timer, bin number) lives on the customer record and there was no way to reach it from this screen. It opens the ordinary Edit Customer popup rather than a second form, so there is only ever one place those fields are saved.'],
  [45, 'Warehouse', 'Bundles when feet are missing', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers".\n3. Click "All Customers".\n4. Find a customer who already has a price set and click Edit on their record.\n5. Clear out the box called "Measured Feet" completely, so it is empty.\n6. Click Save.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse", then click the tab called "Build", and check whether this customer is listed there.\n8. If they are not in the build queue, go back and click Edit on their record again, change one of their light colours, and click Save - that will put them in the queue.\n9. On a phone, open the Crew Portal (the app/site the installation crew uses).\n10. Sign in to the Crew Portal.\n11. Go to the section called "Warehouse".', 'The crew should still see a bundle estimate listed for that house, worked out from their price since their measured feet is missing. It should show a real estimated number, not a blank space and not a zero.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [46, 'Warehouse', 'Warehouse extras', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse".\n3. Click the tab called "Build".\n4. Near the top of the page, find the box labeled "Add From Inbox".\n5. Leave the first box - the one asking which house this is for - completely blank.\n6. Leave "What is this for?" set to "Lights". The Pattern is now PICKED, not typed - there is no text box any more. Click the "+ Red" button and then the "+ Pure White" button, and check that two numbered chips appear underneath reading "1. Red" and "2. Pure White". Click "+ Red" once more, then click the small x on that third chip to take it off again.\n7. In the "How many bundles" box, type the number 3.\n8. Leave the Wire Color box on "Not set - buffer stock" for now.\n9. Click the button that says "Add to Queue".\n10. Scroll down to the queue below and find the buffer stock group that matches the colours you just picked. Press its "Show items" button to open it.\n11. On the row for the entry you just added, click the button that says "Edit".\n12. The form at the top of the page should fill in with what you picked - the chips should come back showing "1. Red" and "2. Pure White" - and its heading should now say "Edit This Build". Change the Wire Color to "Green", and leave everything else alone.\n13. Click the button that says "Save Changes".\n14. Look at the queue again and find your entry - check which group it is sitting in now, and what the small grey line under it says.\n15. Open that group and, in the count box on it, type the number 1 and click "Mark Completed".\n16. Look at the queue again and check what that entry now shows.\n17. Complete the remaining 2 of that same entry.', 'Editing must change the entry you clicked Edit on - it must NOT create a second one, so there should still be only the one entry you made. After saving, the small grey line under it should read "Green wire", and it should have moved out of the "buffer stock (wire not set)" group into the group for that colour pattern on green wire - alongside any real houses with those same colours on green wire. Then, after completing 1 out of the 3, the entry should still be sitting in the queue showing 2 remaining. It should NOT disappear or get deleted just because 1 was completed. Only after all 3 have been completed should it disappear.\n\nOn the Pattern picker itself: clicking a colour should add it to the end of the list, clicking the same colour twice should give you two chips (that is how a repeating pattern like Warm White, Red, Red, Warm White is built), and the small x on a chip should remove just that one. There should be no way to type into the Pattern field at all.', 4,'', '', 'Entries added by hand can now be corrected instead of only deleted - every one of them has an Edit button - and the form now asks for a Wire Color, which it never did before, so anything added by hand could only ever say "wire not set". Picking a house fills the wire in from their record, the same way the pattern and the bundle count already do.\n\nUpdated 2026-08-15: the Pattern is now PICKED from colour buttons instead of typed into a text box. That is the change to check this time - steps 6 and 12, and the note at the end of what to expect. The reason is that whatever was typed became the heading the whole build queue is grouped under, so one stray spelling made a whole extra group with its own bundle count.'],
  [47, 'Warehouse', 'Recycle queue membership', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers".\n3. Click "All Customers".\n4. Click Edit on a test customer\'s record.\n5. Find the setting called "RSVP" (this records whether the customer wants the Christmas lights service again) and set it to "No".\n6. Click Save.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse".\n8. Click the tab called "Recycle".\n9. Leave them in the list for now. Go back to Customers, click Edit on that same test customer, change nothing except their phone number, and click Save.\n10. Return to Warehouse and click the tab called "Recycle" again, and press the "Show houses" button on the group to open it.', 'After step 8 that test customer should appear in the Recycle list once you open the group with the "Show houses" button - listed by name, with their customer number beside it. After the plain save in step 9 they should still be sitting there exactly once - saving their record must not add them a second time or change anything about their place in the list.', 3, '', '', 'Two changes: the recycle flag is only raised when the RSVP actually changes to No (not re-worked-out on every save), and the group now has a real "Show houses" button with the customer number shown next to each name.'],
  [48, 'Warehouse', 'Mark recycled returns the number', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse".\n3. Click the tab called "Recycle".\n4. Press the "Show houses" button on a group to open it, and pick any one customer sitting in that list.\n5. Write down their customer number - it is shown on their row.\n6. Mark that customer as recycled.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the words "Customer Numbers".\n8. Click the tab called "Available Pool".\n9. Look for the number you wrote down in step 5.\n10. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers".\n11. Click "All Customers" and find that same customer\'s record.\n12. Click Edit on that record, open Edit Customer again, change nothing except their phone number, and click Save.\n13. Go to Warehouse and click the tab called "Recycle".', 'The customer number you wrote down should now be sitting in the Available Pool, ready to be given to someone else. That same customer\'s own record should no longer show any customer number at all. After the plain save in step 12 they should NOT come back into the Recycle list - their lights have already been pulled, so there is nothing left to recycle.', 3, '', '', 'Marking someone recycled is unchanged, but a later save on that record used to put them straight back in the Recycle queue. The group is also opened with a "Show houses" button now, and the customer number is printed next to each name. Please run this again with the two new steps at the end.'],
  [49, 'Warehouse', 'Back next year does not recycle', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers".\n3. Click "All Customers".\n4. Click Edit on a test customer\'s record.\n5. Find the setting called "RSVP" (this records whether the customer wants the Christmas lights service again) and set it to "Back Next Year".\n6. Click Save.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse".\n8. Click the tab called "Recycle".\n9. Look through the list for that test customer.\n10. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the words "Customer Numbers".\n11. Click the tab called "All Numbers".\n12. Check whether that test customer still has a customer number listed.', 'That test customer should NOT appear anywhere in the Recycle list, and they should still have the same customer number listed in All Numbers. Only setting RSVP to a flat "No" should trigger recycling - "Back Next Year" should not.', 2, '', '', 'How the recycle flag gets written changed, so this needs confirming again. The answer should be exactly the same as before - Back Next Year must still never recycle anyone.'],
  [50, 'Warehouse', 'Build-ahead goals', '1. On a phone, open the Crew Portal (the app/site the installation crew uses).\n2. Go to the section called "Warehouse".\n3. Set a build-ahead goal by entering a colour pattern and how many of that pattern you want built.\n4. Record some progress against that goal (enter a number showing how many have been built so far).\n5. Close the Crew Portal completely.\n6. Reopen the Crew Portal.', 'The build-ahead goal and the progress you recorded should both still be there after reopening the portal - nothing should have reset. Note this known gap: there is currently no way to see or set these build-ahead goals from admin at all; they only exist inside the Crew Portal.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [51, 'Routes', 'Candidate pool', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard for Highlighting Utah staff — not highlightingutah.com, which is the public website customers see).\n2. Set up a test customer whose RSVP (their yes/no answer about whether they want the lights service) is set to Yes, and who has a complete, valid address.\n3. Set up a second test customer whose RSVP is set to No.\n4. Set up a third test customer who is already included on a route that has already been saved.\n5. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Routes\'.\n6. Click the tab labeled \'Generate Route\'.\n7. Click the tab labeled \'Install\'.\n8. Click the button to generate a new route.\n9. Look at the list of customers the system offers to include on the route.', 'The only customer offered should be the one whose RSVP is Yes and who is not already on a saved route. The customer whose RSVP is No, and the customer who is already scheduled on a saved route, should both be left off the list.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [52, 'Routes', 'Geographic clustering', '1. Log into admin.\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Generate Route\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Install\'.\n5. Generate a route that includes customers from at least two or three different cities.\n6. Read through the list of stops in the order the route puts them in.', 'The stops should be grouped together by area/city, so a crew driving the route isn\'t zig-zagging back and forth between cities. The stops should NOT simply be listed in the order the customers were originally added to the system.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [53, 'Routes', 'Saving creates the route doc', '1. Log into admin.\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Generate Route\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Install\'.\n5. Generate a route, choosing a specific date and a specific crew for it.\n6. Save that route to the calendar.\n7. Click \'Routes\' again in the left menu.\n8. Click the tab labeled \'List View\'.\n9. Look for the route you just saved.\n10. Click the tab labeled \'Calendar\'.\n11. Look for the same route on the date you chose.\n12. Refresh the whole page in your browser (reload it).\n13. Check the List View tab and the Calendar tab again.', 'The route should appear on the correct date, under the correct crew, in both the List View tab and the Calendar tab. After reloading the page, the route should still be there — it should not disappear or reset.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [54, 'Routes', 'Stops are frozen snapshots', '1. Log into admin.\n2. Using the Routes section, save an install route that includes one test customer.\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n4. Click \'All Customers\'.\n5. Find that same test customer and click \'Edit\' on their record.\n6. Change their name to something different.\n7. Change their street address to something different.\n8. Click \'Save\'.\n9. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\' again.\n10. Click the tab labeled \'List View\'.\n11. Open the route you saved in step 2.', 'The saved route should still show the customer\'s OLD name and OLD address, not the new ones you just typed in. This is on purpose — once a route is saved, its stops are frozen so the information a crew is looking at doesn\'t change out from under them in the middle of the day.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [55, 'Routes', 'Resync reaches upcoming routes only', 'AUTOMATED - run-all.js covers four separate rules here: the resync only touches UPCOMING routes, it never blanks a field that did not change, a resync failure cannot lose the customer edit, and past routes are left alone as history. What is left is confirming on screen that a real edit reaches a real upcoming route.\n\n1. Log into admin.\n2. Using the Routes section, save one route with a date in the past, and make sure it includes a test customer.\n3. Save a second route with a date in the future, that includes that same test customer.\n4. Go to Customers, then All Customers, and click Edit on that same test customer.\n5. Change one of their details (for example, their name or address).\n6. Click Save.\n7. Go to Routes and find the option to run the route resync (a tool that pushes updated customer details out to routes).\n8. Run the route resync.\n9. Open the past route from step 2 and look at that customer\'s stop.\n10. Open the future route from step 3 and look at that customer\'s stop.\n11. Compare the two.', 'The future-dated route should show the corrected/updated details you just entered. The past-dated route should still show the old details, unchanged — because it\'s already happened, it\'s treated as history and isn\'t touched by the resync.', 2, '', '', 'The resync rules are automated. Reworded to check one real edit reaching one real upcoming route.'],
  [56, 'Routes', 'Outlet timer on the stop', '1. Log into admin.\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n3. Click \'All Customers\'.\n4. Find a test customer and click \'Edit\' on their record.\n5. Find the field called \'Outlet Timer\' and set it to \'Yes\'.\n6. Click Save.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n8. Click the tab labeled \'Generate Route\'.\n9. Click the tab labeled \'Install\'.\n10. Generate and save a route that includes this test customer.\n11. Open the saved route card (the on-screen display of that saved route, showing the details for each stop).\n12. Without clicking into the customer\'s full record, read the stop for this customer just as a crew member out in the field would.', 'The instruction about the outlet timer should be visible right there on the route card itself, without needing to open the customer\'s record. Note: this is a known gap in the app — the outlet timer information is not currently included in the frozen route stop, so if you don\'t see it on the route card, that confirms the bug that\'s already known about (it is not a new problem).', 1, '', '', ''],
  [57, 'Routes', 'Route card is view-only', '1. Log into admin.\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'List View\'.\n4. Open a saved route.\n5. On the route card, try to change the customer\'s name directly.\n6. Try to change the customer\'s address directly.\n7. Try to change the customer\'s notes directly.\n8. Try to change the customer\'s price directly.\n9. On that same route card, try to set a house rating.\n10. On that same route card, try to set the job status.', 'You should NOT be able to change the customer\'s name, address, notes, or price directly from the route card — those fields should be locked/read-only there, and to change them you\'d need to go to the customer\'s own record instead. The house rating and the job status, however, SHOULD be changeable directly on the route card — those are the only two things you can edit from that screen.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [58, 'Routes', 'Rating saves from the route card', '1. Log into admin.\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'List View\'.\n4. Open a saved route.\n5. On one stop in that route, set a house rating.\n6. Refresh the whole page in your browser (reload it).\n7. Open that same route again.\n8. Check that the house rating is still set on that stop.\n9. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n10. Click \'All Customers\'.\n11. Find that same customer and click \'Edit\' on their record.\n12. Check the house rating on their customer record.', 'The house rating should still be there after refreshing the page and reopening the route. The same rating should also show up on that customer\'s own record under Customers, All Customers, Edit — it should be saved in both places.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [59, 'Routes', 'Job status from the route card', '1. Log into admin.\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'List View\'.\n4. Open a saved route that has at least three stops.\n5. On the first stop, mark its job status as \'Done\'.\n6. On the second stop, mark its job status as \'Needs Fix\'.\n7. On the third stop, mark its job status as \'Removal Done\'.\n8. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n9. Click \'All Customers\'.\n10. Find the first customer and check that their record shows the status \'Done\'.\n11. Find the second customer and check that their record shows the status \'Needs Fix\'.\n12. Find the third customer and check that their record shows the status \'Removal Done\'.', 'All three statuses should have saved correctly and should show up correctly on each customer\'s own record under Customers, All Customers — \'Done\' on the first customer, \'Needs Fix\' on the second, and \'Removal Done\' on the third.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [60, 'Routes', 'Fix route', '1. Log into admin.\n2. Mark one test customer as needing a fix. You can do this either from a route card (the on-screen display of a saved route), or from the Crew Portal (the separate portal that installation crews use out in the field).\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Generate Route\'.\n5. Click the tab labeled \'Fixes\'.\n6. Click the button to generate the route.\n7. Look at the list of customers the system offers to include.', 'Only houses that have been flagged as needing a fix should be offered. Normal, already-completed houses that don\'t need a fix should NOT appear in this list.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [61, 'Routes', 'Removal route', '1. Log into admin.\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Generate Route\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Removal\'.\n5. Click the button to generate a removal route.\n6. Look at the list of customers the system offers to include.\n7. Pick two or three of the customers on that list.\n8. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n9. Click \'All Customers\'.\n10. Find each of those two or three customers and check their records.', 'Only houses that actually still have their lights up and are due for removal should be offered on the removal route. When you check those customers\' records under Customers, All Customers, their details should confirm they truly do have lights up and are due for removal.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [62, 'Routes', 'Calendar, Map and Take Downs views', '1. Log into admin.\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'List View\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Generate Route\'.\n5. Click the tab labeled \'Calendar\'.\n6. Click the tab labeled \'Map View\'.\n7. Click the tab labeled \'Take Downs\'.\n8. Pick one route that has already been saved.\n9. Look for that same route in the List View tab.\n10. Look for that same route in the Calendar tab.\n11. Look for that same route in the Map View tab.\n12. Look for that same route in the Take Downs tab (if it applies to that type of route).', 'All five tabs (List View, Generate Route, Calendar, Map View, Take Downs) should open without any error messages. The same saved route you picked should be findable across the relevant views — they\'re all showing the same underlying routes, just displayed in different ways (a list, a calendar, a map, and so on).', 1, '', '', ''],
  [63, 'Routes', 'Reorder route tabs', '1. Log into admin.\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n3. Look at the row of tabs (List View, Generate Route, Calendar, Map View, Take Downs).\n4. Click and hold on the \'Calendar\' tab, then drag it to a different position in that row, then let go.\n5. Refresh the whole page in your browser (reload it).\n6. Check the order of the tabs again.\n7. Sign out of admin completely.\n8. Sign back into admin.\n9. Click \'Routes\' again and check the tab order one more time.', 'The new tab order you set by dragging the \'Calendar\' tab should still be there after refreshing the page, and it should still be there after signing out and signing back in. This means the tab order is saved to your personal login, not just remembered by the computer or browser you happen to be using.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [64, 'Crew Portal', 'Staff login then pick your name', '1. On a phone, open a web browser and go to the Crew Portal website (employee.html — this is the private site crew members use, different from the public highlightingutah.com site and different from admin).\n2. Sign in using the shared staff login (the one login/password that all crew members use to get into the Crew Portal).\n3. After signing in, you will see a list of employee names. Tap your own name (or the test employee\'s name) from that list.', 'The portal should open as that person, with their name showing and only the tabs that person is allowed to see visible. No other tabs should appear.', 2, '', '', 'The "who are you" screen is now a list of names to tap instead of a text box. Typing a first name used to match the FIRST person with that name \u2014 two people called Jose and the second became the first, pay rate included.'],
  [65, 'Crew Portal', 'Role template controls tabs', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard — not the public website, and not the Crew Portal).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word \'Responsibilities\'.\n3. Near the top of the Responsibilities page, click the tab that says \'Role Templates\'.\n4. Create a new template. Turn on (check) some of the tab checkboxes and leave others off (unchecked) — this template decides which tabs a crew member can see in the Crew Portal.\n5. Still in the Responsibilities section, click the tab that says \'Employee Names\'.\n6. Find a test employee and apply (assign) the template you just created to them.\n7. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html, a separate website crew members use) on a phone or computer.\n8. Sign in as that same test employee.', 'In the Crew Portal, that employee should see only the tabs that were turned on in the template you created. Every tab you left off in the template should not appear at all.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [66, 'Crew Portal', 'Per-employee tab visibility', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Responsibilities\'.\n3. Click the tab that says \'Employee Names\'.\n4. Click on one employee to open their individual settings.\n5. Tick some of the individual tab checkboxes on and untick others off, just for this one person.\n6. Click \'Save\'.\n7. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html, a separate website for crew members) and sign in as that same employee.', 'That employee should see only the exact tabs you ticked on in step 5, and nothing else. Also check that no other employee\'s tab access changed because of this — everyone else should still see whatever they saw before.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [67, 'Crew Portal', 'Today\'s Route loads', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Create and save an install route for today\'s date, assigned to a specific crew (a group of employees who work together).\n3. On a phone, open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as one of the employees on that crew.\n4. Tap the button or tab labeled \'Today\'s Route\'.', 'The list of stops (houses) for the route should load in the same order the office set for the route. Everything on the screen should be easy to read and easy to tap with a finger, and you should not need to pinch to zoom in or scroll sideways to see anything.', 2, '', '', 'IMPORTANT \u2014 removal routes now appear in the Crew Portal. They never did before, so every day of January showed "No route scheduled for this day". Check a removal day loads and is headed Take Downs.'],
  [68, 'Crew Portal', 'Live fields re-read', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Create and save a route for today\'s date that includes a test customer.\n3. On a phone, open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and go to \'Today\'s Route\'.\n4. Find that same test customer\'s stop and read what it currently shows.\n5. Go back to admin (you can use a computer for this part).\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n7. Click \'All Customers\'.\n8. Find that same test customer and click \'Edit\'.\n9. Change the house notes to something new.\n10. Change (upload a new) photo for that house.\n11. Click \'Save\'.\n12. On the phone, refresh (reload) the \'Today\'s Route\' page in the Crew Portal.', 'After refreshing, the crew member should see the brand-new note and the brand-new photo on that stop, even though the rest of that stop\'s information stays the same as when the route was first saved (the route itself is \'frozen\' at the time it was made, but notes, outlet info, fix notes, and photos always show the latest, current version).', 1, '', '', ''],
  [69, 'Crew Portal', 'Mark done', '1. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and go to \'Today\'s Route\'.\n2. Find a house on the route and mark it \'Done\' (a button or checkbox labeled Done).\n3. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n4. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n5. Click \'All Customers\'.\n6. Find that same house in the list.', 'That house should show as completed (Done) in admin. This test matters more than it looks — the automatic nightly billing only sends an invoice for houses that are marked Done, so if this does not work, customers will not get billed.', 2, '', '', 'Mark Done now writes the correct field for the kind of route: an install marks the house installed, a REMOVAL marks it taken down. This matters for billing \u2014 check that marking a removal done does NOT re-invoice the customer.'],
  [70, 'Crew Portal', 'Needs fix with note and photo', '1. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and go to \'Today\'s Route\'.\n2. Find a house and flag it as needing a fix (a repair — something broken that needs a follow-up visit).\n3. Type a note describing the problem.\n4. Attach a photo of the problem.\n5. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n7. Click \'All Customers\'.\n8. Find that same house and open it.\n9. Also, in the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n10. Click \'Generate Route\'.\n11. Click the tab labeled \'Fixes\'.', 'On that house\'s page in admin, you should be able to see the fix note and the photo you just added. Also, when you generate a Fixes route, that same house should now appear in the list of houses needing a fix.', 2, '', '', 'Flagging an issue now asks what is wrong and refuses an empty answer, and the note shows on the card. A flag holds up that customer\u2019s whole invoice, so it needs a reason.'],
  [71, 'Crew Portal', 'One-time note clears on done', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Find a test house and add a \'one-time note\' — a note meant to apply only to this single upcoming visit, not to every future visit.\n3. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and go to \'Today\'s Route\'.\n4. Find that test house\'s stop and confirm the one-time note is showing on it.\n5. Mark that house \'Done\'.\n6. Go back to admin and open that same house\'s page again.', 'The one-time note should now be gone (cleared, empty) — it was only supposed to apply to that one visit, so it should not still be there and should not show up again on the customer\'s next visit.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [72, 'Crew Portal', 'Timers tab', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n3. Click \'All Customers\'.\n4. Click \'Edit\' on one test customer, set the \'Outlet Timer\' setting to \'Yes\', and click \'Save\'.\n5. Click \'Edit\' on a second test customer, set the \'Outlet Timer\' setting to \'No\', and click \'Save\'.\n6. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and go to the tab labeled \'Timers\'.', 'The customer you set to \'Yes\' should appear in the Timers list, and the customer you set to \'No\' should NOT appear in that list. The Timers list should only ever contain houses whose Outlet Timer setting is \'Yes\'.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [73, 'Crew Portal', 'Clock in and out', '1. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as a test employee.\n2. Tap \'My Timecard\'.\n3. Tap the button to clock in.\n4. Wait a few minutes.\n5. Tap the button to clock out.\n6. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Time Logs\'.\n8. Click the tab labeled \'Time Log History\'.', 'The shift you just worked should be listed with the correct employee name, the correct start (clock-in) time, the correct end (clock-out) time, and the correct total length of the shift (matching roughly how many minutes passed between clocking in and out).', 1, '', '', ''],
  [74, 'Crew Portal', 'Daily checklist', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Responsibilities\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Daily Responsibilities\'.\n4. Make sure a checklist exists for the role (job type) that your test employee has.\n5. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as that test employee.\n6. Tap the tab labeled \'Checklist\'.\n7. Tick off (check) a few of the items on the checklist.\n8. Fully close the Crew Portal (close the browser tab or app completely, not just navigate to another page).\n9. Reopen the Crew Portal and sign in again as that same test employee.', 'The items you ticked off should still be ticked, for that same employee and that same day. Also check that those same ticks do not show up as checked when a different employee opens their own checklist — each person\'s checkmarks should be separate.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [75, 'Crew Portal', 'Time-off request', '1. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as a test employee.\n2. Find and use the option to submit a time-off request.\n3. Fill in the dates you want off.\n4. Fill in a reason.\n5. Submit the request.\n6. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Responsibilities\'.\n8. Click the tab labeled \'Time Off\'.', 'The time-off request should be listed there with the correct employee name, the correct dates, and the correct reason you typed in.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [76, 'Crew Portal', 'Note to the office', '1. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as a test employee.\n2. Find the option to send a note to the office and send one.\n3. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n4. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Responsibilities\', and also check \'Customer Messages\' — look in both places to find where crew notes show up.', 'The note you sent should show up in admin, attached to (labeled with) the correct employee\'s name — the one who actually sent it.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [77, 'Crew Portal', 'Warehouse crew views', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Make sure there is at least one house sitting in the Warehouse Build list (houses that need light bundles built for them).\n3. Make sure there is at least one house sitting in the Recycle list (houses whose lights need to be taken back and reused).\n4. Open the Crew Portal (employee.html) and sign in as an employee who has warehouse access.\n5. Open the \'Warehouse\' section.\n6. Go through the build list and check the houses on it.\n7. Go through the recycle list and check the houses on it.\n8. Go through the timers list and check the houses on it.\n9. Go through the pull lists and check the houses on it.', 'Each of the four lists (build, recycle, timers, pull) should load with the correct, right houses on it. Also confirm you can actually take action on items from the phone — for example, marking a house as \'built\' or marking it as \'recycled\' — and that the action actually works.', 2, '', '', 'The crew route screen changed: finished stops collapse, there is a "Hide finished stops" toggle, a phone link on each stop, and an offline badge in the top bar.'],
  [78, 'Invoices', 'One invoice per payer', 'RETIRED - fully automated, nothing left for a person. Which invoice a customer belongs to is decided by custInvoiceKey in js/money.js and invoiceKeyFor in functions/index.js. run-all.js unit-tests that rule four ways (phone digits win, punctuation is ignored, a customer with no phone falls back to a lowercased email, and someone with neither gets no key), and money-parity.test.js proves the admin copy and the server copy file every customer under the SAME key. Mark this N/A. The row is kept rather than deleted so the history stays readable.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard - this is different from highlightingutah.com, which is the public website customers see).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen. Click the word \'Customers\'.\n3. Click the button that says \'Add a Customer\'.\n4. Fill in a phone number for this test customer.\n5. Fill in a price for this test customer.\n6. Save this customer.\n7. Click \'Add a Customer\' again to add a second test customer.\n8. This time, fill in an email address but leave the phone number box blank.\n9. Fill in a price for this second test customer.\n10. Save this second customer.\n11. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click the word \'Invoices\'.\n12. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n13. Find both of the test customers you just added in this list.', 'Both test customers should have an invoice. The customer who has a phone number should have their invoice filed under their phone number. The customer who has no phone number (only an email) should have their invoice filed under their email address instead.', 3, '', '', 'Now fully covered by automated tests, including a check that the admin and server copies agree. Mark it N/A.'],
  [79, 'Invoices', 'Amount owed formula', 'AUTOMATED - the formula (install + removal + changeFees - credits - deposit, floored at zero) runs on every commit in run-all.js, and money-parity.test.js proves the admin copy and the nightly billing copy agree across ~13,000 combinations. What a person still needs to check is that the right number REACHES THE SCREEN - no unit test can see that.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click the word \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n4. Click on any one test invoice to open it.\n5. On paper, write down these five numbers from the invoice: the install amount, the removal amount, the change fees amount, the credits amount, and the amount already paid.\n6. On paper, add together the install amount, the removal amount, and the change fees amount.\n7. From that total, subtract the credits amount.\n8. From that new total, subtract the amount already paid. This final number is your hand-calculated balance.\n9. On the invoice itself, add a new credit that is bigger than whatever balance is still owed.\n10. Look at the balance shown on the invoice again.', 'The balance shown on the invoice screen should match the number you calculated by hand in step 9. After you add a credit bigger than the remaining balance in step 10, the balance should show as $0 - it should not go below $0 (no negative numbers).', 4, '', '', 'Money now shows cents everywhere, and balances are compared in whole cents so a fully paid invoice can no longer read "Partial Payment" against a $0.00 balance. Confirm the invoice card reads like $1,234.50.'],
  [80, 'Invoices', 'New member $30 fee applied once', 'AUTOMATED - looksLikeNewMember is tested directly on every commit: the fee follows the ticked checkbox, an unset or unticked box never charges, and the decision is proved to no longer read createdAt. run-all.js also checks the admin preview and the nightly function agree about who counts as new. What is left is confirming a real invoice shows the $30 once and not twice.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Add a brand new test customer, filling in a price for them, and tick the \'Charge $30 new member installation fee\' box before saving. That box is off by default and it is now the only thing that charges the fee.\n3. Mark this test customer\'s house as \'Done\'.\n4. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n5. Click the \'Nightly Automation\' tab.\n6. Click the button that says \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n7. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n8. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n9. Find this test customer\'s invoice and write down the dollar amount shown.\n10. Go back to Invoices, then the Nightly Automation tab, and press \'Send Invoices Now\' a second time.\n11. Go back to Invoices, then Invoice List, and check this test customer\'s invoice amount again.', 'The $30 new-member fee should be added to the invoice the first time you press \'Send Invoices Now\', making the amount go up by $30. After pressing \'Send Invoices Now\' a second time, the amount should stay exactly the same as it was after the first time - the $30 should not be added again. If you had left the \'Charge $30 new member installation fee\' box unticked in step 2, no $30 should ever appear - that is now the correct behaviour, not a bug.', 5, '', '', 'The nightly run was losing the $30 join fee whenever it re-added up a customer with more than one house. Re-check the fee on a multi-house invoice.'],
  [81, 'Invoices', 'Admin only predicts the fee', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Add a brand new test customer and fill in a price for them, ticking the \'Charge $30 new member installation fee\' box before saving. That box is off by default and it is now the only thing that makes the fee appear anywhere.\n3. Do NOT mark this test customer\'s house as \'Done\' - leave that status alone.\n4. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n5. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n6. Find this test customer and click on their invoice card to open it.', 'The invoice card should show the $30 new-member fee, but labeled as a prediction or an expected amount rather than something already charged. It should be clear from the wording that this fee has not actually been added to their invoice yet - it only actually gets added once the nightly automatic run happens.', 3, '', '', 'The $30 fee now shows up only for customers who have the \'Charge $30 new member installation fee\' box ticked - it is no longer guessed from what year they enrolled. Step 2 now tells you to tick that box, otherwise there is nothing for the card to predict.'],
  [82, 'Invoices', 'Imported customers are never charged the join fee by accident', 'AUTOMATED - run-all.js tests looksLikeNewMember directly, including the exact bulk-import case (a record stamped with this year\'s createdAt and an untouched checkbox), and fails if the fee decision ever reads createdAt again. This was the ~945-customer overcharge risk, so it is worth knowing it is now guarded on every commit. A person still needs to confirm a real imported customer\'s invoice shows no $30.\n\n1. For this test you need a customer whose record came in through the bulk import (the batch upload of the whole customer list), not one you typed in by hand.\n2. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n3. Find that customer\'s record and click \'Edit\' on them.\n4. Check the \'Charge $30 new member installation fee\' box - confirm it is NOT ticked, and leave it unticked. Close the popup.\n5. Give that customer a price.\n6. Mark that customer\'s house as \'Done\'.\n7. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n8. Click the \'Nightly Automation\' tab.\n9. Click the button that says \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n10. Go to Invoices, then Invoice List, and open this customer\'s invoice.\n11. Check the dollar amount on their invoice.', 'The invoice should be their price and nothing more - NO $30 should have been added. This is the important one to get right: the bulk import gave every customer the same enrollment date, so any rule that decides "new member" by looking at when someone enrolled would treat almost the entire customer list as new and quietly add $30 to nearly every invoice. If you do see an extra $30 here, stop and report it - that is real money going out wrong.', 4, '', '', 'The overcharge guard is now an automated test. Reworded to check one real imported customer rather than the logic.'],
  [83, 'Invoices', 'Invoice amount syncs back to the customer', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer who pays only for their own single house (not someone who pays for several houses at once).\n3. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n4. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n5. Find that customer\'s invoice and change the \'Amount\' field on it to a new number.\n6. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Customers\'.\n7. Click \'All Customers\'.\n8. Find that same customer and click \'Edit\' on their record.\n9. Compare the house price shown on the Edit screen to the amount you just typed into their invoice.', 'The house price on the Edit Customer screen should match the amount you just typed into the invoice. If that customer had the $30 new-member fee added to their invoice, the $30 should be subtracted back off first before it\'s saved as the house price - so the two numbers can never drift apart from each other.', 3, '', '', 'Saving an invoice used to wipe the removal charge and could cut a recorded payment down to the install amount. Check that a takedown charge and a payment both survive an open-and-save.'],
  [84, 'Invoices', 'Group invoice - per-property prices, not a typable total', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Find a payer who covers several houses - meaning several different customer records are all billed to the same phone number.\n3. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n4. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n5. Find that payer\'s invoice and click \'Edit\' on it.\n6. Try to click into the \'Amount\' box and type a number directly into it.\n7. Instead, find the \'Price per property\' list on the panel and change the number in just one of the property rows.\n8. Watch the \'Amount\' box while you do this.\n9. Click \'Save Changes\'.\n10. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Customers\', then \'All Customers\', and find the one house whose row you changed.', 'The \'Amount\' box should be greyed out and refuse to accept typing - it is read-only. Changing a number in one property row should update the \'Amount\' box immediately by itself, adding up to the new total (including the $30 new-member fee as its own line if this payer has one). After clicking Save Changes, that one house\'s price on its own customer record in All Customers should match the new number you typed, and the other houses in the group should be unchanged.', 4, '', '', 'On a group invoice the payment box now accepts up to the full charge, including removal and any light-change fees, instead of the install total alone.'],
  [85, 'Invoices', 'Bill-to grouping totals', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Customers\'.\n3. Click \'All Customers\'.\n4. Pick three test houses and set all three to be billed to the same phone number.\n5. Give each of the three houses a different price, and write all three prices down on paper.\n6. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n7. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n8. Find the invoice for that shared payer (phone number).', 'There should be exactly one invoice for that payer, not three separate invoices. The install amount on that one invoice should equal the three house prices added together.', 3, '', '', 'Payer-group behaviour changed — a multi-house invoice now has editable per-property prices and a read-only total.'],
  [86, 'Invoices', 'Editing a payer price keeps the group', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Use the same group of three test houses (billed to the same phone number) from the previous test.\n3. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Dashboard\', then \'Invoices\', then the \'Invoice List\' tab, and write down that payer\'s current invoice total.\n4. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Customers\'.\n5. Click \'All Customers\'.\n6. Find just one of the three houses in that group and change its price.\n7. Click Save.\n8. Go back to Invoices, then Invoice List, and open that same payer\'s invoice.', 'The invoice total should have been recalculated using all three houses\' current prices, not just the one you changed. All three houses should still be listed on the invoice - none of them should have dropped off.', 4, '', '', 'The resync that rebuilds a group invoice was failing and writing nothing at all. Run this one end to end and confirm the total actually changes.'],
  [87, 'Invoices', 'Switching bill-to clears the old invoice', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Customers\'.\n3. Click \'All Customers\'.\n4. Find a customer who currently pays for their own house (not part of a group) and who has an outstanding light-change fee showing on their invoice (a $30 fee charged when a member changes their light colors late in the season).\n5. Click \'Edit\' on that customer.\n6. Change their billing so they are now billed to somebody else\'s phone number instead of their own.\n7. Click Save.\n8. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n9. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n10. Search for the old payer (the original customer) in the list.\n11. Search for the new payer (the phone number you switched them to) in the list.', 'The old invoice under the original customer\'s own name should be gone or emptied out - there should not be a leftover invoice still billing them separately for the same house. The light-change fee that was on their old invoice should NOT have disappeared - it should now show up on the new payer\'s invoice instead, listed as its own separate fee line.', 2, '', '', 'Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path.'],
  [88, 'Invoices', 'Referral credit stacks', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n4. Open any one test invoice.\n5. Add a referral credit to this invoice.\n6. Add a second, separate referral credit to the same invoice.\n7. Read the invoice card carefully.', 'Each referral credit should be worth $25. With two of them added, they should add up to $50 off the total. Each referral credit should show up as its own separate line on the invoice, rather than the two being merged together into a single $50 line.', 2, '', '', 'Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path.'],
  [89, 'Invoices', 'Service adjustment credit', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n4. Open any one test invoice.\n5. Add a service adjustment credit. Type in your own dollar amount for it.\n6. Type in your own reason/note explaining the credit.\n7. Read the invoice card, including the balance shown.', 'The balance on the invoice should drop by exactly the dollar amount you typed in. The credit should show up on the invoice as its own separate line, with the reason you typed written on it.', 2, '', '', 'Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path.'],
  [90, 'Invoices', 'Credit never goes below zero', 'AUTOMATED - run-all.js proves a credit larger than the bill still reads Paid in Full and never produces a negative balance. What is left is the visible half: that the leftover lands as carryover credit on the customer and is not silently lost.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Find a test invoice belonging to a customer who has already paid in full, or has almost paid in full.\n3. Add a credit to that invoice that is larger than whatever small amount is still owed.\n4. Read the balance shown on the invoice.\n5. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Customers\'.\n6. Click \'All Customers\'.\n7. Find that same customer and click \'Edit\' on their record.', 'The balance should stop at $0 and never go into negative numbers. The leftover amount of the credit (the part that didn\'t fit against the balance) should show up on the Edit Customer popup as \'carryover credit\' to be used next time. Nothing should be refunded to the customer.', 2, '', '', 'The floor-at-zero rule is now automated. Reworded to cover the carryover behaviour only.'],
  [91, 'Invoices', 'Carryover shows on Edit Customer', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Use the same test customer from the previous test (the one who ended up with carryover credit).\n3. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Customers\'.\n4. Click \'All Customers\'.\n5. Find that customer and click \'Edit\' on their record.\n6. Look for the carryover credit amount on this popup, along with the note that explains it.\n7. Try clicking to clear (remove) that carryover credit.', 'Both the carryover credit amount and its explanatory note should be visible on the Edit Customer popup. You should be able to clear the carryover credit from this same popup if it was added by mistake.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [92, 'Invoices', 'Carryover auto-applies once', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer who currently has carryover credit banked on their account.\n3. Mark that customer\'s house as \'Done\'.\n4. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n5. Click the \'Nightly Automation\' tab.\n6. Click the button that says \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n7. Check that customer\'s invoice balance.\n8. Also check how much carryover credit they have remaining.\n9. Click \'Send Invoices Now\' a second time.\n10. Check the invoice balance and the remaining carryover amount again.', 'The carryover credit should come off the customer\'s balance one time only, on the first run. Any leftover carryover credit (if the whole amount wasn\'t used) should still be banked for next time. Pressing \'Send Invoices Now\' a second time should not apply the carryover credit all over again - both numbers should stay the same as after the first run.', 2, '', '', 'Nightly Automation and Start New Season moved out of the Automation Emails panel into their own tabs under Invoices - the steps were rewritten for the new path.'],
  [93, 'Invoices', 'Status agrees in both places', 'RETIRED - fully automated, nothing left for a person to do. money-parity.test.js lifts computeInvoiceStatus out of js/money.js and computeInvoiceStatusServer out of functions/index.js, runs BOTH over roughly 13,000 money combinations plus blank, text and broken values, and fails the moment they disagree. It also checks custInvoiceKey and invoiceKeyFor file a customer under the same invoice. It runs on every commit and blocks the Cloud Functions deploy if it fails, so the office screen and the nightly billing run can no longer drift apart unnoticed. Mark this N/A. The row is kept rather than deleted so the history of why it went away stays readable - the same way test 96 was handled.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Set up three test invoices: one where nothing has been paid, one where part of it has been paid, and one that has been paid in full.\n3. On at least one of these three invoices, add both a credit and a change fee (the $30 fee for a late light-color change).\n4. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n5. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n6. Write down the status word shown on each of the three invoice cards (for example: Unpaid, Partial, or Paid in Full).\n7. Send each of the three customers an invoice email (use \'Send Invoices Now\').\n8. Open the email that arrives for each of the three and read the status word written in it.', 'For all three test invoices - including the one with the credit and change fee on it - the status word (Unpaid, Partial, or Paid in Full) shown on the invoice card in admin should say exactly the same thing as the status word shown in the email that gets sent out.', 4, '', '', 'Both copies of the balance formula — the office screen and the nightly billing run — now compare whole cents.'],
  [94, 'Invoices', 'Invoice list and filters', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n4. Wait for the full list to finish loading.\n5. Pick two or three invoices from the list and check their totals against the actual customer prices for those same customers.\n6. Use the filter options near the top of the screen to narrow down the list.', 'Every invoice in the list should load with the correct total matching the customer\'s price. Each filter option should actually shorten the list down to only the invoices matching what that filter says it does.', 2, '', '', 'Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path.'],
  [95, 'Invoices', 'Import / Export', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the \'Import / Export\' tab.\n4. Click the option to export to Excel.\n5. Open the downloaded Excel file.\n6. Pick five rows from the file.\n7. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab and find those same five customers\' invoice cards.\n8. Compare each of the five rows in the file against its matching invoice card.', 'Every number in the exported Excel file should match what is shown on the invoice cards - no rows should be missing, and no totals should be different between the file and the cards.', 4, '', '', 'The invoice export now has its own Tips column. Tips are real money charged to a card and were missing from every export, so anything handed to a tax preparer understated income.'],
  [96, 'Invoices', 'Season billing emails (retired)', 'RETIRED - there is nothing left to test here. The Season Billing Emails tab was removed when Invoices became its own item in the left menu. The same job is done in Automation Emails > Templates: write the wording as a template in the Billing folder and use Send to Selected. Score this one N/A.', 'Nothing to check. Mark it N/A - the screen it covered no longer exists. The row is kept rather than deleted so the history of why it went away stays readable.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [97, 'Invoices', 'Health Check resync totals', 'AUTOMATED - the Health Check engine is executed by run-all.js against real drift cases, including the two that used to cause false alarms: a multi-house invoice is NOT flagged as drift, and the $30 new member fee is not mistaken for it, while genuine price drift IS caught. What is left is pressing the button and confirming the totals it writes are right.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Find a test invoice that already has the $30 new-member fee added to it.\n3. Write down the total shown on that invoice.\n4. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Health Check\'.\n5. Click the button that says \'Run Health Check\'.\n6. Find the section about totals on the Health Check results.\n7. Click the resync/fix button in that totals section.\n8. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n9. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n10. Find that same test invoice and check it again.', 'The invoice total should have been corrected by the resync, and the $30 new-member fee should still be included in it - the resync/fix tool must never quietly remove the fee while correcting the total.', 4, '', '', 'The "Resync totals" button was throwing an error and writing nothing every time it was pressed. Confirm it now repairs the total.'],
  [98, 'Fees & Locks', 'Colour change charges $30', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard for Highlighting Utah — not the public website).\n2. Pick a test customer who has not changed their light colours in the last two days.\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Invoices\'.\n4. Click \'Invoice List\'.\n5. Find that test customer\'s invoice and write down their invoice total.\n6. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as that test customer.\n7. Click the tab called \'Light Colors\'.\n8. Change the colours to something different.\n9. Click \'Save\'.\n10. Go back to admin and open that same customer\'s invoice card (their invoice in the Invoice List).', 'You should see a new $30 change fee has been added to the invoice. It should show up as its own separate line item labeled as a fee — it should NOT be mixed in with (added on top of) the install price so that you can\'t see it separately.', 2, '', '', 'Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path.'],
  [99, 'Fees & Locks', 'Base price edit does not wipe the fee', 'AUTOMATED - run-all.js proves the light-change fee is held in its own changeFees total, separate from install, and that it counts toward the balance. That is what stops a base-price edit wiping it. What is left is doing it once on a real invoice and seeing the fee still listed afterwards.\n\n1. Use the same test customer from the previous test (\'Colour change charges $30\'), who now has a $30 change fee on their invoice.\n2. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n4. Click \'All Customers\'.\n5. Find that test customer in the list and click \'Edit\' on them.\n6. Change the house price (the base price for their service) to a different number.\n7. Click \'Save\'.\n8. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Invoices\'.\n9. Click \'Invoice List\'.\n10. Open that test customer\'s invoice.', 'The $30 change fee should still be there on its own separate line on the invoice. Changing the house price should not remove or wipe out the change fee.', 3, '', '', 'The separation of changeFees from install is automated. Reworded to check one real invoice.'],
  [100, 'Fees & Locks', '48-hour free window', '1. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as a test customer.\n2. Go to the Light Colors tab and change their light colours.\n3. Click \'Save\'. This charges a $30 fee and opens up a 48-hour period where the customer can change colours again for free — this is called the \'free window.\'\n4. Look at what the portal says for when the free window ends, and write down that time.\n5. Right away (without waiting), change their colours a second time.\n6. Click \'Save\' again.\n7. Log into admin (the private office dashboard) and check that test customer\'s invoice.', 'The second colour change (the one you made right after the first, inside the free window) should not add a second $30 fee. Only one $30 change fee should show on the invoice.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [101, 'Fees & Locks', 'First change after the window charges again', '1. Find a test customer whose last colour change that was charged a $30 fee happened more than 48 hours ago.\n2. Log into admin (the private office dashboard) and write down that customer\'s invoice total.\n3. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as that same test customer.\n4. Go to the Light Colors tab and change their light colours.\n5. Click \'Save\'.\n6. Go back to admin and check that customer\'s invoice total again.\n7. In the Member Portal, note the new free-window end time (the time until which they can change colours again for free).', 'Another $30 change fee should have been charged, and the invoice total should be $30 higher than before. A brand new 48-hour free window should have started counting from the moment of this most recent change, not from the earlier one.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [102, 'Fees & Locks', 'Portal warns before charging', '1. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as a test customer who is currently outside their free window (meaning a colour change right now would cost $30).\n2. Click the tab called \'Light Colors\'.\n3. Start changing the colours.\n4. Before clicking \'Save\', read whatever message appears on the screen.\n5. Click \'Save\' (or whatever button confirms the change) to confirm.\n6. Read the message the portal shows after you confirm.', 'Before you confirm, the portal should show a warning that tells you this change will cost $30 and ask you to confirm before it charges you. After you confirm, the portal should tell you the exact date and time the new free window will end.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [103, 'Fees & Locks', 'Fee is enforced server-side', '1. This test checks that the $30 fee is charged by Highlighting Utah\'s computer system (the \'server\') itself, not just something the website shows on the screen in your browser — so it can\'t be tricked or skipped.\n2. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as a test customer who is currently outside their free window (meaning a colour change right now would cost $30).\n3. Change their light colours.\n4. When the warning about the $30 fee appears, confirm it.\n5. Log into admin (the private office dashboard) and check that the $30 fee landed on that customer\'s invoice.\n6. If you have access to someone technical who can try making the same colour change while skipping or bypassing the warning message in the browser, have them try that.', 'The $30 fee should be added to the invoice no matter what — whether the warning was confirmed normally, or whether someone tried to skip the warning in the browser. This proves the fee is applied by Highlighting Utah\'s server (its central computer system), not by anything happening in the customer\'s browser, so it can\'t be avoided by tricking the browser.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [104, 'Fees & Locks', 'Assignment lock blocks install routes', '1. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as a test customer.\n2. Change their light colours and confirm the $30 fee. This starts a 48-hour \'lock\' that is meant to keep them off install routes for two days (a \'route\' is the list of stops a work crew visits in a day).\n3. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n4. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n5. Click \'Generate Route\'.\n6. Click the tab called \'Install\'.\n7. Generate a route (click whatever button creates/builds the route).\n8. Look through the list of customers the app is offering to put on this route, and check whether that test customer is in it.\n9. Now try to add that test customer to the route yourself, by hand.\n10. Try to save the route.', 'The app should not offer that customer automatically in the list for the install route. If you try to add them by hand anyway and save, the app should stop you (hold them back) and tell you why — because they are inside their 48-hour lock period.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [105, 'Fees & Locks', 'Fix and removal routes ignore the lock', '1. Use the same locked customer from the previous test (\'Assignment lock blocks install routes\'), who is currently inside their 48-hour lock period.\n2. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n4. Click \'Generate Route\'.\n5. Click the tab called \'Fixes\'.\n6. Generate a route (click whatever button creates/builds the route) and check whether that customer is offered or allowed on it.\n7. Click the tab called \'Removal\'.\n8. Generate a route and check whether that customer is offered or allowed on it.', 'That customer should be eligible to be included on both the Fixes route and the Removal route, even though they are locked. The 48-hour lock should only ever block them from install routes, not from Fixes or Removal routes.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [106, 'Fees & Locks', 'Test routes bypass the lock', '1. Use the same locked customer from the earlier tests, who is currently inside their 48-hour lock period.\n2. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n4. Generate a \'Test\' route (a route type used for testing) and make sure to include that locked customer on it.', 'That customer should be allowed onto the Test route with no warning or blocking message. This confirms that Test routes can always include a customer regardless of whether they\'re locked.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [107, 'Fees & Locks', 'Change after assignment flags the office', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Build and save an install route (a list of stops for a work crew) that includes a test customer.\n3. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as that same test customer.\n4. Change their light colours and save the change.\n5. Go back to admin.\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customer Messages\'.\n7. Open the folder called \'System\'.\n8. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n9. Click \'List View\'.\n10. Open the route you saved in step 2.', 'In the System folder under Customer Messages, there should be a note called \'Lights Changed After Assignment\' waiting for you, telling you to remove that customer from the route and add them back. On the route card (the saved route you opened), a badge (a small visual marker) should be showing on that customer\'s stop.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [108, 'Fees & Locks', 'Assigning clears the flag', '1. Use the same flagged customer from the previous test (\'Change after assignment flags the office\'), who currently has the \'Lights Changed After Assignment\' note and badge.\n2. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n4. Remove that customer from the saved route.\n5. Add that customer back onto a route.\n6. Save the route.\n7. Open the route card again (open that saved route).', 'Both the badge on their stop and the \'Lights Changed After Assignment\' flag/note should now be gone.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [109, 'Fees & Locks', 'Remove light-change fees', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click \'Invoice List\'.\n4. Open an invoice that has a light-change fee on it.\n5. Write down the balance (the amount currently owed) shown on that invoice.\n6. Click the button labeled \'Remove light-change fee(s)\'.\n7. Look at the balance again.', 'The change-fee line(s) should be gone from the invoice, and the balance should have dropped by exactly the amount of the fee(s) that were removed — no more, no less.', 2, '', '', 'Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path.'],
  [110, 'PayPal', 'Order amount is the real balance', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Add a light-change fee to a test customer\'s invoice.\n3. Also add a credit (a discount or amount subtracted from what they owe) to that same invoice.\n4. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as that test customer.\n5. Look at the balance (amount owed) shown in the portal and write it down. It should already include both the fee you added and the credit you added.\n6. Start a PayPal payment from the portal.\n7. Add a tip to the payment.\n8. Before confirming, read the total amount that PayPal is asking you to pay.', 'The amount PayPal asks for should equal the balance you wrote down from the portal, plus the tip you added — nothing more and nothing less. This confirms the change fee is actually included in the amount PayPal charges, not just something the portal displays on screen without really charging for it.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [111, 'PayPal', 'Capture records the payment', '1. Use the same test customer from the previous test (\'Order amount is the real balance\').\n2. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Invoices\'.\n4. Click \'Invoice List\'.\n5. Find that customer\'s invoice and write down their current \'amount paid\'.\n6. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as that customer and complete a small real PayPal payment.\n7. Go back to admin and check that customer\'s invoice again.', 'The \'amount paid\' on the invoice should have gone up by exactly the amount that was paid through PayPal, and the balance (amount still owed) should have dropped to match.', 3, '', '', 'Every payment now also writes a line to a payment log. After a card payment, open that customer\u2019s invoice in Invoices \u2192 Invoice List and check the new "Payment history" section shows the amount, the date, and "Card (PayPal)".'],
  [112, 'PayPal', 'Webhook is the safety net', '1. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as a test customer.\n2. Start a small PayPal payment from the portal.\n3. Complete the payment on PayPal\'s own website/screen.\n4. As soon as the payment is complete, immediately close the browser tab — do not wait for it to send you back to the portal.\n5. Wait a few minutes.\n6. Log into admin (the private office dashboard) and check that customer\'s invoice.', 'The payment should still show up as recorded on the invoice, even though you closed the browser tab and it never sent you back to the portal to confirm.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [113, 'PayPal', 'Tips tracked separately', '1. Sign in to the Member Portal (the website customers use to manage their own account) as a test customer.\n2. Make a payment that includes a tip added on top.\n3. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n4. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Invoices\'.\n5. Click \'Invoice List\'.\n6. Open that customer\'s invoice.\n7. Also, in the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Dashboard\' again, then click \'Financial Overview\'.', 'The tip amount should be recorded separately from the regular service payment money — it should show up on its own, not mixed in with what was paid toward the job. The tip should NOT count toward reducing the balance the customer owed for the actual Christmas-light job.', 2, '', '', 'Invoices is now its own item in the left menu instead of a tab inside Dashboard - the click-by-click steps were rewritten, so this needs running against the new path.'],
  [114, 'Nightly Automation', 'Runs at 7 PM Mountain', '1. Log into the admin dashboard (the private office website you log into — not highlightingutah.com, which is the public site customers see).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click the words \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n4. Find the switch labeled \'Nightly Invoice Automation\' and check that it is switched to the ON position.\n5. Make sure at least one test house (a customer record you\'re using for testing) is marked \'Done\' and has not been billed yet.\n6. Leave the app alone and wait until after 7:00 PM.\n7. After 7:00 PM, go back to the \'Nightly Automation\' tab and look at the list labeled \'Last 10 nightly runs\'.', 'That evening, an invoice email should have gone out for the test house, and the run you\'re checking for should be listed in the \'Last 10 nightly runs\' list along with the time it ran.', 3, '', '', 'Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house.'],
  [115, 'Nightly Automation', 'Bills only completed and unbilled houses', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Set up three test houses (test customer records): House A marked \'Done\' and never billed; House B not marked \'Done\'; House C marked \'Done\' but already billed.\n3. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n5. Click the button labeled \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n6. Check each of the three test houses\' email history to see which ones received an email.', 'Only House A — the one marked \'Done\' and not yet billed — should have received an invoice email. House B and House C should not have received anything.', 3, '', '', 'Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house.'],
  [116, 'Nightly Automation', 'Picks the right template', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Set up two test houses, both marked \'Done\': House A already paid in full, House B still owing money.\n3. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n5. Click the button labeled \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n6. Open the email that arrives for House A.\n7. Open the email that arrives for House B.\n8. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Automation Emails\', click the tab labeled \'Templates\', then click \'Billing\' to see the two email templates stored there.\n9. Compare House A\'s email against the two templates, and compare House B\'s email against the two templates.', 'House A (paid in full) should have received the email that matches the \'Paid Receipt\' template. House B (still owing) should have received the email that matches the \'Unpaid\' template. They should not both have received the same template.', 3, '', '', 'Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house.'],
  [117, 'Nightly Automation', 'amount_paid shows real payment', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Open a test invoice.\n3. Cover most of the balance using a credit (not an actual payment), so the customer owes nothing but has barely paid any real money.\n4. Mark that customer\'s house as \'Done\'.\n5. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n6. Click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n7. Click the button labeled \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n8. Open the email that arrives for this test customer.\n9. Find the line in the email that shows the amount paid.', 'The \'amount paid\' line in the email should show only the real money the customer actually handed over — not the full job total, and not the amount that was covered by the credit. A credit-covered invoice must never claim the customer paid money they never actually paid.', 3, '', '', 'Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house.'],
  [118, 'Nightly Automation', 'credit_lines token', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Automation Emails\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Templates\'.\n4. Click \'Billing\'.\n5. Open one of the billing email templates.\n6. Inside the template\'s text, type the placeholder text {{credit_lines}} at the spot where you want any credits to show up.\n7. Save the template.\n8. Add a credit to a test invoice.\n9. Mark that test customer\'s house as \'Done\'.\n10. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Invoices\', then click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n11. Click the button labeled \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n12. Open the email that arrives for this test customer.', 'The credit should appear in the email as its own separate line, along with the reason for the credit, so the customer can see exactly why their total is lower than expected.', 3, '', '', 'Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house.'],
  [119, 'Nightly Automation', 'fee_lines token', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Automation Emails\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Templates\'.\n4. Click \'Billing\'.\n5. Open one of the billing email templates.\n6. Inside the template\'s text, type the placeholder text {{fee_lines}} at the spot where you want any fees to show up.\n7. Save the template.\n8. Add a light-change fee (the $30 fee charged when a customer changes their light colors late) to a test invoice.\n9. Mark that test customer\'s house as \'Done\'.\n10. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Invoices\', then click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n11. Click the button labeled \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n12. Open the email that arrives for this test customer.', 'The $30 light-change fee should show up in the email as its own separate line, so it isn\'t a surprise to the customer when they see the total.', 3, '', '', 'Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house.'],
  [120, 'Nightly Automation', 'Run is logged', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n4. Click the button labeled \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n5. Scroll down to the list labeled \'Last 10 nightly runs\'.', 'The run you just triggered should appear at the very top of the \'Last 10 nightly runs\' list, showing how many invoices were sent, how many were skipped, and how many had errors.', 3, '', '', 'Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house.'],
  [121, 'Nightly Automation', 'Summary text arrives', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n4. Find the box labeled \'Text a nightly billing summary to this number\' and type your own phone number into it.\n5. Click the \'Save\' button.\n6. Click the button labeled \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n7. Watch your phone for a text message.', 'A text message should arrive on your phone with a short one-line summary, like \'23 sent, 0 errors\'. If this text ever stops arriving on a normal night, it means the nightly run did not fire.', 3, '', '', 'Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house.'],
  [122, 'Nightly Automation', 'Stale-run banner', '1. This is something you watch for happening on its own — you don\'t need to trigger it yourself.\n2. Log into the admin dashboard.\n3. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n5. Confirm the switch labeled \'Nightly Invoice Automation\' is turned ON.\n6. Look at the list labeled \'Last 10 nightly runs\'.\n7. Check the date and time of the newest (most recent) run in that list. If it is more than about a day and a half old, look at the top of that card on the screen.', 'If the nightly run hasn\'t happened in over about a day and a half, a red warning banner should appear at the top of the card, telling you that billing is turned on but hasn\'t actually run. That banner is the backup warning for the whole nightly system being dead.', 3, '', '', 'Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house.'],
  [123, 'Nightly Automation', 'Toggle off stops the run', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n4. Find the switch labeled \'Nightly Invoice Automation\' and switch it to the OFF position.\n5. Make sure a test house is marked \'Done\' and has not been billed.\n6. Wait until after 7:00 PM.\n7. After 7:00 PM, check that test house\'s invoice to see if it was emailed.\n8. Also check the \'Last 10 nightly runs\' list.', 'No invoice email should have gone out for the test house, and no new run should appear in the \'Last 10 nightly runs\' list. Turning the toggle off should mean the nightly billing does not run at all.', 3, '', '', 'Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house.'],
  [124, 'Nightly Automation', 'Missing EmailJS keys fail loudly', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Automation Emails\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'EmailJS Setup\' (the keys still live here - only the nightly run itself moved).\n4. Before changing anything, copy the EmailJS keys (the boxes holding the connection codes for the email-sending service) somewhere safe, like a text file, so you can put them back afterward.\n5. Clear out (delete) the EmailJS keys from their boxes.\n6. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n7. Click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n8. Click the button labeled \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n9. Read whatever message appears on the screen after clicking that button.\n10. Go back to Automation Emails, then EmailJS Setup, and immediately put the keys back exactly as you copied them.', 'You should get a clear error message on the screen saying the invoices could not be sent. The app must not act like everything worked fine when the email keys are missing.', 3, '', '', 'Nightly billing now runs per payer, not per house.'],
  [125, 'Start New Season', 'Check First writes nothing', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Start New Season\'.\n4. Use the dropdown menu to pick which customers to reset.\n5. Click the button labeled \'Check First\' (this only shows you a preview — it does not actually change or save anything).\n6. Read the list of results that appears.\n7. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n8. Click \'Invoice List\'.\n9. Find two of the customers from the list in step 7 and check their invoices.', 'The list should show exactly which customers would be reset and for how much money. The two invoices you check afterward should be completely unchanged — nothing should actually have been written or saved yet.', 2, '', '', 'Nightly Automation and Start New Season moved out of the Automation Emails panel into their own tabs under Invoices - the steps were rewritten for the new path.'],
  [126, 'Start New Season', 'Last year payments are banked', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Dashboard\'.\n3. Click \'Financial Overview\'.\n4. Write down the season total shown there.\n5. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n6. Click the tab labeled \'Start New Season\'.\n7. Run it (actually run it, not just \'Check First\').\n8. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Dashboard\' again.\n9. Click \'Financial Overview\' again.', 'Before Start New Season resets everyone\'s invoices, last year\'s payments should already be added into the Dashboard\'s total. When you check Financial Overview again afterward, nothing should be missing — the total should show that last year\'s payments were banked, not lost.', 3, '', '', 'Start New Season now SAVES A FULL SNAPSHOT of the season\u2019s payment records before it changes anything \u2014 who paid, how much, when and how, per customer. Check the confirm box mentions it, and that after running, Finance \u2192 Snapshots shows this year with a customer count and a Download button.'],
  [127, 'Start New Season', 'Price minus the join fee', '1. Before running anything, log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Pick a returning customer (someone who was a customer last year and is expected to continue).\n3. Find and write down last year\'s invoice amount for that customer.\n4. Note whether that invoice amount included the one-time $30 join fee (the fee charged once when a customer first joins).\n5. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n6. Click the tab labeled \'Start New Season\'.\n7. Run it.\n8. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n9. Click \'Invoice List\'.\n10. Find that same customer in the list.', 'The customer\'s new invoice should charge the same amount as last year, minus the one-time $30 join fee, since they already joined once and shouldn\'t be charged to join a second time.', 2, '', '', 'Nightly Automation and Start New Season moved out of the Automation Emails panel into their own tabs under Invoices - the steps were rewritten for the new path.'],
  [128, 'Start New Season', 'Discounts cleared, carryover kept', '1. Before running anything, log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Find a customer who has both: a discount on their invoice, and a carryover credit (leftover credit saved on their customer record from a previous season) on their customer record.\n3. Write down the discount amount.\n4. Write down the carryover credit amount.\n5. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n6. Click the tab labeled \'Start New Season\'.\n7. Run it.\n8. Check that customer\'s invoice.\n9. Open \'Edit Customer\' for that same customer.', 'Last year\'s discount should be gone from the new invoice. The carryover credit amount should be completely unchanged and still sitting on the customer\'s record, ready to be used.', 2, '', '', 'Nightly Automation and Start New Season moved out of the Automation Emails panel into their own tabs under Invoices - the steps were rewritten for the new path.'],
  [129, 'Start New Season', 'Safe to run twice', '1. Log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Start New Season\'.\n4. Run it.\n5. Write down the new invoice amounts for two or three customers.\n6. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Dashboard\', and write down the total shown there.\n7. Go back to \'Invoices\', then the \'Start New Season\' tab, and run it again immediately, a second time.\n8. Check those same two or three customers\' amounts again.\n9. Check the Dashboard total again.', 'Nothing should have changed on the second run. The customer amounts and the Dashboard total should be exactly the same as after the first run — no one should be charged twice, and no money should be banked twice.', 3, '', '', 'The reset now also clears two leftovers from last season on every invoice: the duplicate-receipt guard and the payment-import history. Without that, a customer paying the same total as last year got no receipt, and the first import of the new season silently skipped rows.'],
  [130, 'Start New Season', 'Customers re-bill on reinstall', '1. After running \'Start New Season\', log into the admin dashboard.\n2. Pick a customer whose invoice was just reset by Start New Season.\n3. Mark that customer\'s house as \'Done\'. You can do this either from the Crew Portal (the app crew members use in the field) or from a route card (the stop for that house on a route list).\n4. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click \'Invoices\'.\n5. Click the tab labeled \'Nightly Automation\'.\n6. Click the button labeled \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n7. Check that customer\'s email.\n8. Check that customer\'s invoice.', 'The customer should have been billed again for the new season the moment their house was marked \'Done\' — an invoice email should have gone out and their invoice should reflect the new season\'s charge.', 2, '', '', 'Nightly Automation and Start New Season moved out of the Automation Emails panel into their own tabs under Invoices - the steps were rewritten for the new path.'],
  [131, 'Inbox & Templates', 'System folder collects automatic notices', '1. Open the Member Portal (the private website customers log into to manage their own account) and sign in as a test customer.\n2. Find the setting for light colours, change it to a different colour, and save the change.\n3. Find the setting for wire colour, change it to a different colour, and save the change.\n4. Find the question about the outlet timer, change the answer, and save the change.\n5. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customer Messages\'.\n7. Find the folder labeled \'System\' (it should be pinned near the top of the list) and click it to open it.', 'All three notices - one for changing light colours, one for changing wire colour, and one for changing the outlet timer answer - should have filed themselves automatically into the System folder in Customer Messages, not into the main Inbox where they\'d clutter things up.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [132, 'Inbox & Templates', 'Decisions stay in the main Inbox', '1. Open a new tab in your web browser and go to highlightingutah.com (this is the public website that customers see).\n2. Find the public contact form on the site and send a test message through it.\n3. Open the Member Portal (the private website customers log into to manage their own account) and sign in as a test customer.\n4. Find the option to submit a cancellation request, and submit one.\n5. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customer Messages\'.\n7. Look at the main Inbox and note what messages are sitting there.\n8. Click into the folder labeled \'System\' and look at what\'s in there too.', 'Both the contact-form message and the cancellation request should show up in the main Inbox, not in the System folder. Anything that needs a decision from you must never get buried in System - it should always land in the main Inbox where you\'ll actually see it.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [133, 'Inbox & Templates', 'Drag between folders', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customer Messages\'.\n3. Pick any one message sitting in one folder, and drag it with your mouse into a different folder.\n4. Refresh the whole browser page (reload it completely).\n5. Open the folder the message used to be in, and check whether it\'s still there.\n6. Open the folder you dragged the message into, and check whether it\'s there.', 'The message should now be sitting in the new folder you dragged it into, and it should no longer be in the old folder. After refreshing the page, it should still be in the new folder - the move actually saved, it didn\'t just change on screen temporarily.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [134, 'Inbox & Templates', 'Email templates and folders', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Automation Emails\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Templates\'.\n4. Create a new folder to hold email templates.\n5. Inside that new folder, create a new email template.\n6. Edit the wording of that template.\n7. Save the template.\n8. Go to a place in admin where templates get used - for example, in the left menu click \'Automation Emails\', stay on the \'Templates\' tab, and open the \'Send to Selected\' flow, where a template gets picked.\n9. Check whether your new test template shows up there and can be selected.\n10. Go back to Automation Emails, then Templates, and delete the test template you created.\n11. Delete the test folder you created.', 'Creating the folder, creating the template inside it, editing its wording, and saving should all work without errors. The new template should actually appear as a selectable choice wherever templates get used, like the Send to Selected flow. Deleting the test template and the test folder afterward should also work cleanly, with nothing left behind.', 3, '', '', 'Template folders changed: folders can now be deleted and renamed, and can be nested inside another folder. Re-run it, and this time also make a subfolder inside your test folder and check the parent folder shows its templates too.'],
  [135, 'Inbox & Templates', 'Saved templates and custom codes', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Automation Emails\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Templates\'.\n4. Create a template (or use an existing one) and save it.\n5. Create a custom discount code.\n6. Refresh the whole browser page (reload it completely).\n7. Go to a place in admin where a discount can be applied to a customer or invoice.\n8. Try applying the custom discount code you just created.', 'Both the saved template and the custom discount code should still exist after refreshing the page - neither one should have disappeared. The discount code should actually work when you try to apply it - it should be accepted and applied, not rejected or missing.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [136, 'Health & Data', 'Health Check runs', 'AUTOMATED - run-all.js runs the Health Check engine itself, confirms all 14 checks are present, and confirms that clean data reports nothing at all (a checker that always finds something is a checker nobody reads). What is left is the screen: that the report renders and the fix buttons appear only on the unambiguous checks.\n\n1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Health Check\'.\n3. Find the checkbox labeled \'Hide checks that pass\' and untick it (uncheck it) so every check shows, not just the failing ones.\n4. Click the button labeled \'Run Health Check\'.\n5. Read down through the entire list of checks that appears, top to bottom.', 'Health Check should finish running without throwing any errors, and it should show what it found for every single check in the list. Running it should not change any of your actual data on its own - only the buttons you press yourself, not Health Check itself, are allowed to change data.', 3, '', '', 'Health Check now runs on its own a few seconds after login and every ten minutes, so the count badge beside it in the menu appears without anyone pressing anything. There is also a new check for customers with no map pin, and one for customers with no email address.'],
  [137, 'Health & Data', 'Import Center', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Import Center\'.\n3. Prepare a small Excel file with just a couple of rows of payments in it, so you can check the numbers by hand afterward.\n4. Import that Excel file\'s payments through Import Center.\n5. Open the folder that the imported payments went into.\n6. Compare each imported amount against the matching amount in your original Excel file.', 'The imported batch of payments should be sitting in the correct folder, and every single amount should match exactly what was in the Excel file, down to the penny.', 2, '', '', 'The Import Center card that just told you to go and ask a chatbot has been replaced with a real tool: it gives every existing customer an opening line in the new payment log, and it has a "Check First" that writes nothing.'],
  [138, 'Health & Data', 'Member Export', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Member Export\'.\n3. Run the export.\n4. Open the file that downloads.\n5. Count how many rows of customers are in that file.\n6. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n7. Click \'All Customers\' and find the total customer count shown there.\n8. Compare the row count from the exported file against that total customer count.', 'The exported file should have the columns you\'d expect for customer information, and the number of rows in the file should match the total number of customers shown under Customers, then All Customers.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [139, 'Health & Data', 'Full Backup', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Site Settings\'.\n3. Find the card labeled \'Full Backup\'.\n4. Click the button that says \'Download Full Backup\'.\n5. Open the file that downloads.\n6. Skim through what\'s inside the file.', 'The file should download successfully, and when you open it, it should contain your customers, invoices, quotes, routes, employees, and settings - not just one or two of those categories, but all of them.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [140, 'Health & Data', 'No silent permission errors', 'RETIRED - fully automated, nothing left for a person. This test asked you to hunt the browser console for permission errors. run-all.js now does it properly and on every commit: it checks that EVERY Firestore collection the code touches has a matching rule in firestore.rules, and that every where+orderBy query has a composite index in firestore.indexes.json. A missing rule is the exact cause of a section silently rendering empty, so this is caught before it ships rather than found by accident. Mark this N/A.\n\n1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Open your browser\'s console: press the F12 key (on a Mac, press Cmd+Option+I instead).\n3. In the panel that opens, click the tab labeled \'Console\'.\n4. Clear out anything already shown in the console (look for a clear or trash-can button, or right-click inside the console and choose \'Clear console\').\n5. Starting at the top of the left-side menu in admin, click every single item in the menu one at a time, working your way down to the bottom.\n6. For any item that has sub-tabs, click into every one of those sub-tabs too.\n7. Keep watching the console the entire time you are clicking through everything.', 'No red permission-denied errors should appear in the console at any point while clicking through the menu. This matters because if part of the database is missing from the security rules, that section of admin just looks empty on screen instead of showing an obvious error - the console is the only place you\'d actually catch it.', 2, '', '', 'Rules-and-indexes coverage is now checked automatically on every commit. Mark it N/A.'],
  [141, 'Employees & Payroll', 'Add an employee', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Responsibilities\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Employee Names\'.\n4. Add a test person: give them a role, a pay type, and set the admin flag (the setting controlling whether they\'re an admin) however you want it for this test.\n5. Save the new test person.\n6. Open the Crew Portal (the website crew members log into).\n7. Look at the picker where you choose which employee\'s name you are.', 'The test person should appear in the employee roster in admin, and their name should also show up as a choice in the Crew Portal\'s name picker.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [142, 'Employees & Payroll', 'Role templates', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Responsibilities\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Role Templates\'.\n4. Create a new template and choose a specific set of tabs for it (pick whichever tabs you want to test with).\n5. Click the tab labeled \'Employee Names\'.\n6. Apply the template you just created to one test employee.\n7. Apply that same template to a second, different test employee.\n8. Open the Crew Portal (the website crew members log into) and sign in as the first test employee.\n9. Write down which tabs you see for them.\n10. Sign out of the Crew Portal.\n11. Sign back in as the second test employee.', 'Both test employees should see exactly the same set of tabs in the Crew Portal, and that set of tabs should match what you chose in the Role Template.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [143, 'Employees & Payroll', 'Weekly payroll', '1. Make sure a few work shifts have already been clocked in through the Crew Portal (the website crew members log into) - clock some in yourself as a test if needed.\n2. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Time Logs\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Weekly Payroll\'.\n5. Pick a specific week to look at.\n6. Pick one person and add up the hours shown for them that week.\n7. Click the tab labeled \'Time Log History\' and check that same person\'s actual clocked shifts for that week against the hours you just added up.\n8. Find someone in the payroll list who worked both warehouse shifts and crew shifts that week, and check their total pay.', 'The hours in Weekly Payroll should add up correctly to match the actual clocked shifts in Time Log History. For the person who worked both warehouse and crew shifts, their hours should be split out by the correct pay rate for each kind of work, not just lumped together into one total.', 2, '', '', 'Payroll now refuses to export a week whose timecards were not loaded, instead of quietly exporting fewer hours than were worked. Page back several weeks and confirm you get a clear red message rather than an empty table.'],
  [144, 'Employees & Payroll', 'Time Log History', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Time Logs\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Time Log History\'.\n4. Use the filters to narrow the list down to just one specific person and one specific week.\n5. Pick a few of the shifts shown and check the date, start time, end time, and length against what that person actually worked.', 'The dates, times, and shift lengths shown should all be correct, and after applying the filters, the list should genuinely only show that one person\'s shifts from that one week - not shifts from other people or other weeks mixed in.', 2, '', '', 'Time Logs now loads by date (two seasons back) instead of stopping at the most recent 300 shifts. An older week used to look identical to a week nobody worked.'],
  [145, 'Employees & Payroll', 'Timecard change request', '1. Open the Crew Portal (the website crew members log into) and sign in as a test employee.\n2. Submit a correction request for one of that employee\'s shifts.\n3. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n4. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Time Logs\'.\n5. Find the change request you just submitted.\n6. Before approving anything, click the tab labeled \'Time Log History\' and check what that shift currently shows.\n7. Go back and approve the change request.\n8. Check the same shift in Time Log History again.', 'The shift should show its original, unchanged values right up until you approve the request. Only after you approve it should the shift\'s values actually change to match the correction.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [146, 'Employees & Payroll', 'Crews config', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Responsibilities\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Crews\'.\n4. Set how many crews there should be.\n5. Assign people to each crew.\n6. Save your changes.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n8. Click \'Generate Route\'.\n9. Click \'Install\'.\n10. Open the dropdown menu used for picking a crew.', 'The crews and the people you just set up in Responsibilities should be exactly the ones listed when you open the crew dropdown while generating an install route.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [147, 'Employees & Payroll', 'Crew overrides', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Responsibilities\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'One-Day Reassignment\'.\n4. Move one person to a different crew, but only for a single specific day.\n5. Check the crew list for that specific day to confirm the move took effect.\n6. Check the crew list for the day before that day.\n7. Check the crew list for the day after that day.', 'Only the one specific day you changed should show the person on their new crew. The day before and the day after should both still show that person on their normal, regular crew - unaffected by the one-day move.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [148, 'Employees & Payroll', 'Employee categories', '1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Responsibilities\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Employee Names\'.\n4. Create a new category.\n5. Tag one employee with that new category.\n6. Tag a second, different employee with that same category.\n7. Refresh the whole browser page (reload it completely).', 'The category and the tags on both employees should still be there after refreshing the page - nothing should disappear. You should be able to use that category afterward to group or filter the two tagged employees together.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [149, 'Finance', 'Expenses', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard - the private website you use to run the business, not highlightingutah.com, which is the public site customers see).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Dashboard\'.\n3. Click \'Financial Overview\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Manage & Expenses\'.\n5. Look at the total shown on the screen right now, and write it down - this is the total before you make any changes.\n6. Add a new expense (pick any amount) and save it.\n7. Look at the total again and write it down.\n8. Click on the expense you just added so you can edit it.\n9. Change the amount to a different number and save it.\n10. Look at the total again and write it down.\n11. Delete the expense you added.\n12. Look at the total one final time and write it down.', 'Every time you added, edited, or deleted the expense, the total on the dashboard changed to match. Adding it made the total go up by that amount, editing it changed the total by the right amount, and deleting it brought the total back down to what it was before you started.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [150, 'Finance', 'Bills', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Dashboard\'.\n3. Click \'Financial Overview\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Debt & Bills\'.\n5. Add a recurring bill: type in an amount and pick a due date, then save it.\n6. Click the tab labeled \'Budget & Goals\'.', 'The bill you just added shows up in the Debt & Bills list, and it is also counted in the numbers on the Budget & Goals tab - it should not be missing or ignored there.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [151, 'Finance', 'Budget and savings goals', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Dashboard\'.\n3. Click \'Financial Overview\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Budget & Goals\'.\n5. Create a budget category: give it a name, type in a monthly amount, and save it.\n6. Create a savings goal: give it a name, type in a target amount, and save it.\n7. Refresh the page (press F5, or click your browser\'s refresh button).', 'After refreshing, both the budget category and the savings goal are still there - they were saved, not lost. Both should show some kind of progress indicator (a number, bar, or percentage) showing how far along they are toward their monthly amount or savings target.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [152, 'Finance', 'Debt accounts', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Dashboard\'.\n3. Click \'Financial Overview\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Debt & Bills\'.\n5. Add a debt account: give it a name, type in a balance amount, and save it.\n6. Refresh the page (press F5, or click your browser\'s refresh button).', 'After refreshing, the debt account is still listed, and the balance shown next to it matches the exact amount you typed in.', 2, '', '', 'Total revenue and this month’s income now include tips. They were charged to cards and counted in no figure at all, so the Dashboard understated what actually came in.'],
  [153, 'Finance', 'Credit cards and transactions', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Dashboard\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Business Credit Cards\'.\n4. Add a card: give it a name and fill in whatever other details it asks for, then save it.\n5. On that card, log a transaction: type in an amount and save it.\n6. Write down the exact amount you typed for that transaction.\n7. Look at the balance shown for that card.', 'The transaction you logged appears in the list for that card. The card\'s balance changed by exactly the amount of the transaction you logged - not more, not less.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [154, 'Finance', 'Card payments and recurring overrides', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Dashboard\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Business Credit Cards\'.\n4. Pick any card that already has a balance greater than zero.\n5. Record a payment against that card: type in a payment amount and save it.\n6. Set a recurring override for one specific month - this changes the normal recurring amount just for that one month.\n7. Look at the card\'s balance.\n8. Look at the month you set the override for.\n9. Look at the month right before it, and the month right after it.', 'The card\'s balance went down by the exact amount of the payment you recorded. The override you set only shows up on the one specific month you chose - the month before it and the month after it are unaffected and show the normal, non-override amount.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [155, 'Finance', 'Yearly snapshot', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Dashboard\'.\n3. Click \'Financial Overview\'.\n4. Find and click the option to take a year-end snapshot - this saves a copy of the current numbers.\n5. Refresh the page (press F5, or click your browser\'s refresh button).\n6. Open that same snapshot again.', 'The snapshot you took is still there after refreshing, and when you open it again, the figures inside it are exactly the same as when you first took it - nothing has changed or drifted.', 2, '', '', 'The Snapshots list has a new "Payment records" column. A snapshot taken by Start New Season carries the per-customer payment record for that season and can be downloaded as a spreadsheet \u2014 that file is what answers "I paid you in November". Note the delete button beside it now throws away the only remaining copy.'],
  [156, 'Site Content', 'Reviews', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard - the private website you use to run the business, not highlightingutah.com, which is the public site customers see).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Reviews\'.\n3. Add a new review - fill in whatever it asks for (such as a name and review text) - and save it.\n4. Open a new browser tab and go to highlightingutah.com.\n5. Find the reviews section on that page and check whether your new review is there.\n6. Go back to the admin browser tab.\n7. Edit the review you just added - change something in it - and save it.\n8. Go back to the highlightingutah.com browser tab, refresh the page, and check the reviews section again.\n9. Go back to the admin browser tab and delete the review you added.\n10. Go back to the highlightingutah.com browser tab, refresh the page, and check the reviews section one more time.', 'Every time - after adding, after editing, and after deleting - the reviews section on highlightingutah.com matched exactly what was in admin. When you added the review it appeared on the public site, when you edited it the edit showed up on the public site, and when you deleted it, it disappeared from the public site.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [157, 'Site Content', 'Gallery', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Gallery\'.\n3. Add a photo - upload one and save it.\n4. Open a new browser tab and go to highlightingutah.com (the public site customers see).\n5. Find the gallery section and check whether your new photo is there.\n6. Go back to the admin browser tab and remove the photo you just added.\n7. Go back to the highlightingutah.com browser tab, refresh the page, and check the gallery again.', 'Both times you checked, the public gallery on highlightingutah.com matched what admin had. After you added the photo, it showed up on highlightingutah.com. After you removed it, the photo disappeared from highlightingutah.com.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [158, 'Site Content', 'Hero images', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Hero Images\' (these are the large photos shown at the very top of the public homepage).\n3. Either change which images are being used, or change the order they appear in, and save your changes.\n4. Open a new browser tab and go to highlightingutah.com (the public site customers see).\n5. Look at the very top of the homepage.', 'The images at the top of the highlightingutah.com homepage are exactly the ones you set in admin, and they appear in the same order you put them in.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [159, 'Site Content', 'FAQ', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'FAQ\'.\n3. Add a new question and its answer, then save it.\n4. Open a new browser tab and go to highlightingutah.com (the public site customers see).\n5. Find the FAQ section and check whether your new question and answer are there.\n6. Go back to the admin browser tab and edit the question or answer you just added, then save it.\n7. Go back to the highlightingutah.com browser tab, refresh the page, and check the FAQ section again.\n8. Go back to the admin browser tab and delete the question you added.\n9. Go back to the highlightingutah.com browser tab, refresh the page, and check the FAQ section one more time.', 'After every single change - adding, editing, and deleting - the FAQ section on highlightingutah.com matched exactly what was in admin.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [160, 'Site Content', 'Site settings', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word \'Site Settings\'.\n3. Change the hero text (the main headline text shown on the homepage).\n4. Change the contact details (such as phone number or email).\n5. Save your changes.\n6. Add a new city to the service-area list (the list of cities the business serves), and save that too.\n7. Open a new browser tab and go to highlightingutah.com (the public site customers see).\n8. Read the homepage and check that your new hero text is showing there.\n9. Check that the contact details shown on the site match what you typed.\n10. Near the top of the page, find and click the gold button that says \'Free Quote\'.\n11. In the form that pops up, click the dropdown for city.', 'The hero text and contact details you typed into admin are now showing on highlightingutah.com. The new city you added appears as an option in the city dropdown on the Free Quote form.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [161, 'Security', 'Portal hides internal figures', '1. Go to the Member Portal (the part of highlightingutah.com that a real customer logs into to see their own account) and sign in using a real customer\'s information.\n2. Click the \'Payment\' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n3. Click the \'Information\' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n4. Click the \'Light Colors\' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n5. Click the \'Changes\' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n6. Click the \'Contact\' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n7. Click the \'Cancel\' tab and read everything on it carefully.\n8. On every tab, look closely for any of these four things that a customer should NOT be able to see: their customer number, their bin count, their house difficulty rating, or your per-foot pricing (how much you charge per foot of lights).', 'None of those four things - customer number, bin count, house difficulty rating, or per-foot pricing - appear anywhere in the Member Portal, on any of the tabs. The office\'s private business information stays private; the customer only sees their own information, because the server strips those fields out before the page ever gets them.', 2, '', '', 'A quote link could be traded for a customer\'s portal access, home address and gate code. Confirm a quote link shows quote details only.'],
  [162, 'Security', 'Portal writes only through functions', '1. This test needs someone comfortable using a web browser\'s developer console (a technical, built-in browser tool) - ask that person to run it.\n2. Go to the Member Portal and sign in using a real customer\'s phone number and last name.\n3. Using the browser\'s developer console, attempt to write (save) a change directly to the database, instead of using one of the portal\'s own Save buttons.', 'The direct write attempt is refused and blocked. The database will not accept changes unless they go through the portal\'s normal Save buttons, which go through the server. This confirms a customer cannot use the browser console to edit things they aren\'t allowed to edit.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [163, 'Security', 'Rate limiting', '1. Go to the Member Portal.\n2. On purpose, type in a phone number and last name that you know are wrong - not a real customer\'s information.\n3. Click or press the \'Sign In\' button.\n4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 quickly, ten to fifteen times in a row, without waiting between attempts.', 'After several wrong attempts in a row, the portal starts slowing you down - for example, making you wait longer between tries - or it blocks you from trying again, instead of letting you keep guessing forever.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [164, 'Security', 'Portal token minted when missing', '1. In admin, find a customer who has never signed into the Member Portal before and has never received an invoice email before.\n2. Go to the Member Portal and sign in using that customer\'s phone number and last name.\n3. Once their account opens successfully, sign back out of the portal.\n4. Have an invoice email sent to that same customer (ask whoever normally handles this how to trigger it, or wait for the nightly automatic invoice email).\n5. Open that invoice email and click the personalized link inside it - a link made just for that one customer.', 'Signing in with the phone number and last name worked the first time, even though this customer had never used the portal before. The personalized link in the invoice email also works and takes you into the same account. Behind the scenes, a portal token (a private access code) was automatically created for this customer the first time one was needed.', 2, '', '', 'Quote links no longer hand back a customer portal token at all. Confirm that signing in the normal way still works and still creates one.'],
  [165, 'Security', 'Shared staff login is app-side only', '1. This test does not require you to click through anything in the app - it is something to read and confirm you understand.\n2. Read and note this fact: the whole crew shares one single staff login, and then each person picks their own name after logging in.\n3. Understand what that means: any limits on which tabs a particular crew member can see are enforced by the phone app itself (the software), not by the database that stores the actual data.', 'Confirm that you understand this known limitation: anyone who has the shared staff login technically has full database-level access to everything, no matter which tabs the app shows or hides for them. Mark this test \'Pass\' once you have read this and accept it as-is, or mark it \'Fail\' if you think this should be changed.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [166, 'Health & Data', 'Invoice Bulk Update guard', 'AUTOMATED - run-all.js checks that Invoice Bulk Update preserves an existing removal charge and any payments already recorded, which is the whole point of the guard. What is left is running one real bulk update and confirming nothing was lost.\n\n1. Open admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Import Center\'.\n3. Find the box labeled \'Invoice Fields\'.\n4. Pick a real test customer who already has an invoice with at least one of the following on it: a removal charge, a deposit, or a change fee.\n5. Write down the exact removal charge, deposit, and change fee amounts currently sitting on their invoice, before doing anything else.\n6. Into the Invoice Fields box, paste that customer\'s name, their phone number, and a new payment amount that is different from what\'s on the invoice now.\n7. Click the button labeled \'Import / Update Invoices\'.\n8. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Invoices\'.\n9. Click \'Invoice List\'.\n10. Find and open that same test customer\'s invoice again.', 'The install amount on the invoice should have updated to the new payment amount you entered. But the removal charge, deposit, and change fee you wrote down beforehand should all still be exactly the same as before - running this import tool should not quietly wipe them out to zero.', 3, '', '', 'The guard is automated. Reworded to check one real bulk update.'],
  [167, 'Admin', 'Quote photo upload', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Quote Requests".\n3. Find any quote card.\n4. On that card, click the button called "Add Photo" (or "Add More Photos" if a photo is already there).\n5. Upload a house photo directly on the card.\n6. Click the button called "Mark Up Photo".\n7. Try drawing on the photo.', 'The photo you uploaded should appear on the quote card. Clicking Mark Up Photo should open a drawing tool that lets you draw on the photo. This is the manual photo-upload feature that replaced an old Street View lookup tool — it\'s a live feature that\'s actually used, not leftover code, so it should keep working correctly.', 2, '', '', ''],
  [168, 'Admin', 'Bulk Difficulty column', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Customers\'.\n3. Click the tab called \'Bulk Updates\'.\n4. Click the heading \'Customers & Routes\' to open it if it isn\'t already open.\n5. Scroll down to the row of boxes that has \'Wire Color\', \'Outlet Timer\' and \'Use Eaves\'. There should now be a fourth box there called \'Difficulty (optional)\'.\n6. Pick two real customers who are currently Unrated. Write down their street addresses exactly as they appear in Routes.\n7. Paste those two street addresses into the \'Street Address\' box, one per line.\n8. In the \'Difficulty\' box, type Hard on the first line and easy (lowercase, on purpose) on the second line.\n9. Check that the small grey counter above the Difficulty box says \'2 rows\'.\n10. Click \'Import / Update Customers\' and wait for it to finish.\n11. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Routes\'.\n12. Search for each of those two customers and click \'Show Details\' on their card.\n13. Now go back to Bulk Updates and try it again with the same two addresses, but this time type the word Banana in the Difficulty box on the first line.\n14. Click \'Import / Update Customers\' again.', 'After step 10, the first customer\'s difficulty should read Hard and the second should read Easy — typing it in lowercase should still work. Leaving the Difficulty box completely empty should never change anyone\'s existing difficulty. In step 14, the import should refuse to run and show a message naming row 1 and the word Banana, and nothing at all should be imported or changed from that attempt.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [169, 'Invoices', 'Overdue now means 30 days', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Invoices\'.\n3. Click the \'Invoice List\' tab.\n4. Click the button that says \'Filter\'.\n5. Read the wording next to the Overdue tick box.\n6. Tick Overdue and click \'Apply\'.\n7. Look at which customers come back, and check how long ago each of their invoices was last updated.', 'The tick box should read \'Overdue (30+ days unpaid)\', not 14. Only customers whose unpaid invoice is at least 30 days old should appear - someone unpaid for around 20 days should no longer be flagged. This is what makes the list agree with the 30-day due date now printed on the invoice.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [170, 'Invoices', 'Printed invoice shows a number and a due date', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Automation Emails\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Invoices\'.\n4. Tick one customer who still owes money, to show their invoice.\n5. Read the top right corner of the invoice that appears, and then the top left.', 'The top right should show an invoice number starting \'HU-\', the season year, an \'Issued\' date, and a \'Due\' date exactly 30 days after the issued date. The business phone, email and website should appear under the Highlighting Utah name on the left.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [171, 'Invoices', 'Printed invoice has no internal details', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Automation Emails\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Invoices\'.\n4. Tick any one customer to show their invoice.\n5. Read the whole invoice from top to bottom.', 'There should be no customer number anywhere on it, no \'Status: Not yet installed\' line, and no mention of takedown or removal. The old \'Details\' block should be gone entirely and Bill-to should run the full width. It should also no longer claim it is what the nightly email sends, because that was never true.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [172, 'Invoices', 'Printed invoice: a credit is shown once', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer who has a credit on their invoice, or add one to a test invoice.\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Automation Emails\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Invoices\'.\n5. Tick that customer to show their invoice.\n6. Find the credit and count how many times it appears.\n7. Add up the line items and compare the answer against the Total.', 'The credit should appear exactly once, as its own line in the table with its reason. There should be no separate \'Credits\' row down in the totals. The line items should add up to the Total, and the Total less anything already paid should equal the Amount due.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [173, 'Invoices', 'Printed invoice: multi-house shows every property', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Find a payer billed for more than one house (several houses billed to the same phone number).\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Automation Emails\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Invoices\'.\n5. Tick that payer to show their invoice.\n6. Read the line items and the top right corner.', 'Each house should have its own row showing its street address, its own footage and rate underneath, and its own price on the right. The top right should say how many properties the invoice covers. It should NOT show one single footage line taken from the payer\'s own house, which is what made these impossible to check before.', 2, '', '', 'Per-property rows are now editable from the invoice panel.'],
  [174, 'Invoices', 'Printed invoice: paid invoices look different', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer whose invoice is paid in full.\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Automation Emails\'.\n4. Click the tab labeled \'Invoices\'.\n5. Tick that customer to show their invoice.\n6. Compare it against an unpaid customer\'s invoice.', 'On the paid one the \'How to pay\' block should be gone, the due date replaced by the date they paid, the payment status box green, and the bottom line reading \'Amount paid\' rather than \'Amount due\'. The unpaid one should still show How to pay with the portal address, the Venmo handle and cheque wording.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [175, 'Invoices', 'Payment import adds instead of overwriting', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer and record a payment of $200 on their invoice by hand.\n3. Write down the amount paid showing on that invoice.\n4. Build a small test spreadsheet with two columns: their phone number, and the amount 315.\n5. In the menu on the left side of the screen click \'Invoices\', then the \'Import / Export\' tab, and import that file, mapping the phone column and the amount column.\n6. Check that customer\'s invoice again.', 'The amount paid should now be $515 - the $200 already there plus the $315 imported. It should NOT read $315. Before this fix the import replaced the figure instead of adding to it, which quietly lost the earlier payment.', 2, '', '', 'The payment import now writes a line to the payment log for each row it applies, tagged with the file it came from. After an import, open one of those invoices and check the history shows the amount and names the file.'],
  [176, 'Invoices', 'Payment import reaches unpaid customers', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer whose invoice is Unpaid, with nothing paid at all.\n3. Build a test spreadsheet with their phone number and an amount equal to their full balance.\n4. Import that file under \'Invoices\' then \'Import / Export\'.\n5. Check that customer\'s invoice.', 'The payment should have been recorded and the invoice should now read Paid in Full. Before this fix the import only touched invoices already sitting at Partial Payment, so an Unpaid customer who paid in full was silently skipped while the message still said it worked.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [177, 'Invoices', 'Re-importing the same file changes nothing', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Import a small test payment file under \'Invoices\' then \'Import / Export\'.\n3. Write down the amount paid on two or three of the customers in that file.\n4. Import the exact same file a second time.\n5. Check those same customers again.', 'Nothing should have changed on the second import - the amounts should be identical to after the first one. The message should say how many rows were skipped as already imported from that file. Nobody should be credited twice.', 2, '', '', 'Two importer bugs fixed. A CSV amount like "1,234.56" used to import as $1 because the file was split on every comma. And the duplicate guard was per FILE, so a customer with TWO payments in one file silently lost the second — it is per row now. Re-importing the same file is still correctly skipped.'],
  [178, 'Invoices', 'Payment import matches messy phone numbers', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Build a test spreadsheet containing one customer\'s phone number written three ways: with dashes, with brackets and a space, and as plain digits.\n3. Add a small amount next to each.\n4. Add one more row for a customer who has an email address but no phone number, putting their email in the phone column.\n5. Import the file under \'Invoices\' then \'Import / Export\' and read the preview before importing.', 'All three formats of the same number should show as Found in the preview, not only the one that happens to match exactly. The email-only customer should also be found, because their invoice is filed under their email address rather than a phone number.', 2, '', '', 'Amounts now survive currency punctuation — "$1,234.56" reads as 1234.56 rather than 1. Worth re-running with a deliberately messy file.'],
  [179, 'Invoices', 'Billing emails carry a Pay button', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click \'Automation Emails\'.\n3. Click the tab labeled \'Templates\', then open the \'Billing\' folder.\n4. Open the template called \'Nightly Auto-Invoice - Unpaid\' and check it reads \'Pay your invoice here:\' followed by the pay_button token rather than a bare portal_link.\n5. Open the template called \'Nightly Auto-Invoice - Paid Receipt\' and check it uses the view_portal_button token.\n6. Send yourself a test of each and open both emails.', 'The unpaid email should show a gold button reading \'Pay Your Invoice\' with a short line of text introducing it. The receipt should show a button reading \'View Your Portal\' and no \'pay your invoice here\' line, since they owe nothing. Both buttons should open that customer\'s own portal without them having to log in.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [180, 'Invoices', 'Paying in full sends a receipt', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer who has your own email address on their record and still owes money.\n3. Click \'Invoices\' in the left menu, then \'Invoice List\', and find them.\n4. Change their payment status to \'Paid in Full\'.\n5. Check the inbox for that email address.', 'A receipt should arrive using the \'Nightly Auto-Invoice - Paid Receipt\' wording, showing the amount paid and a \'View Your Portal\' button. Admin should also briefly show a \'Receipt emailed\' message.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [181, 'Invoices', 'Paying part sends the balance email', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Pick a test customer with your own email on their record who owes, say, $500.\n3. Click \'Invoices\', then \'Invoice List\', and find them.\n4. Change their payment status to Partial Payment and enter $200 when asked how much they have paid.\n5. Check that inbox.', 'An email should arrive using the new \'Payment Received - Balance Remaining\' template: it should confirm the $200 received, show the invoice total, the amount paid so far, and $300 still due, with \'Pay Your Invoice\' and \'Pay with Venmo\' buttons. It should NOT say they are paid in full.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [182, 'Invoices', 'Small payments and corrections stay quiet', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. On a test customer with your own email, record a payment of $5.\n3. Check the inbox - nothing should arrive.\n4. Now record a payment of $200 and let the receipt arrive.\n5. Change the amount paid back down to $150, as if fixing a typo.\n6. Check the inbox again.\n7. Finally, set the same amount again without changing it.', 'The $5 payment should send nothing, because it is under the $10 minimum. Correcting the figure downward should send nothing. Re-saving the same figure should send nothing. Only the genuine $200 payment should have produced an email.', 2, '', '', 'Corrections are now RECORDED rather than hidden. If you lower a customer\u2019s paid figure, the payment history should show it as a negative "Correction" line \u2014 that is deliberate, because a balance going down is exactly what customers ring up about.'],
  [183, 'Invoices', 'A receipt is never sent twice for one payment', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. On a test customer with your own email, record a payment that clears their balance.\n3. Wait for the receipt to arrive.\n4. Without changing the amount, set the payment status to Paid in Full again.\n5. Check the inbox.\n6. If you can, also pay a test invoice through PayPal in the Member Portal and watch for duplicates.', 'Only one receipt should ever arrive for the same amount paid. The invoice records what figure it last sent a receipt for, which is also what stops a PayPal payment and its background confirmation both emailing the same customer.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [184, 'Invoices', 'Importing payments asks before emailing', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Click \'Invoices\', then the \'Import / Export\' tab.\n3. Import a small test payment file with two or three rows.\n4. Read the question that appears before the import runs.\n5. Choose Cancel on that question the first time, and check nobody was emailed.\n6. Import again and choose OK this time.', 'Before importing it should ask whether to email receipts, and say how many rows the file has so you know how much mail could go out. Cancelling should still import the payments but send nothing. Choosing OK should import and then report how many receipts were emailed.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [185, 'Invoices', 'PayPal payments receipt themselves', '1. Open the Member Portal as a test customer who owes money and has your own email on their record.\n2. Pay through PayPal.\n3. Check that inbox.\n4. Check the invoice in admin.', 'A receipt should arrive without anyone touching admin, because PayPal payments are receipted by the server rather than the browser. If the payment cleared the balance it should be the Paid Receipt wording; if it only covered part, the Balance Remaining one. The invoice should show the payment recorded.', 2, '', '', 'PayPal payments are logged once, not twice, even though both the browser and the PayPal webhook report the same payment. Check a card payment produces exactly ONE line in the payment history.'],
  [186, 'Invoices', 'Send one invoice to yourself', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left, click \'Automation Emails\', then the \'Invoices\' tab.\n3. Find the card headed \'Send yourself one customer\'s invoice\'.\n4. Pick a customer who still owes money, put your own email address in the box, and press \'Send me this invoice\'.\n5. Open your inbox.\n6. Then check that customer in the Invoice List, and ask them if they got anything.', 'You should receive that customer\'s real invoice, with a note at the top saying it is a test copy. The figures should match what their invoice actually says. The customer themselves should receive nothing, their invoice should be unchanged, no $30 fee should have appeared, and they should not be marked as billed.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [187, 'Invoices', 'Test send picks the right template', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Go to \'Automation Emails\', then the \'Invoices\' tab.\n3. Send yourself the invoice of a customer who still owes money.\n4. Now send yourself the invoice of a customer who is paid in full.\n5. Compare the two emails, and read the confirmation line under the button each time.', 'The unpaid customer\'s email should use the \'Nightly Auto-Invoice - Unpaid\' wording with an amount due and a Pay button. The paid one should use the Paid Receipt wording. The line under the button should name which template it used and the amount, so you can tell at a glance which one went.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [188, 'Invoices', 'Fee Check agrees with real invoices', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Click \'Invoices\' in the left menu, then the \'Fee Check\' tab.\n3. Press \'Run all checks\' and read the result.\n4. Now pick a real customer from the Invoice List and note their price, credits, change fees and amount paid.\n5. Type those same numbers into the Fee Check boxes.\n6. Compare the balance and status it shows against what the Invoice List shows for that customer.', 'All 16 checks should come out green. The balance and status from the typed-in numbers should match that real customer\'s row exactly. If they differ, the two are calculating money differently and that needs looking at before anything else.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [189, 'Nightly Automation', 'Two houses on one phone bill together', 'AUTOMATED - the invoice key rule that puts two houses on one phone onto a single bill is unit-tested, and money-parity.test.js proves admin and the nightly run agree on it. run-all.js additionally checks the payer resync totals the whole linked group. What is left is confirming one real shared-phone pair gets one email, not two.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Set up two test houses that share the same phone number (a second home or cabin for the same customer), each with its own price.\n3. Mark the FIRST house \'Done\' only. Look at the menu on the left and click \'Invoices\', then the \'Nightly Automation\' tab, and press \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n4. Check that customer\'s email inbox and their invoice.\n5. Now mark the SECOND house \'Done\' too, and press \'Send Invoices Now\' again.\n6. Check the inbox and the invoice again.', 'After step 3 (only one house done), no invoice email should go out yet — billing waits until every house is finished. After step 5 (both houses done), exactly ONE invoice email should arrive, with the combined total of both house prices and a separate line for each address. The invoice amount in admin should equal the sum of both house prices.', 2, '', '', 'The grouping rule is automated. Reworded to check one real shared-phone pair.'],
  [190, 'Nightly Automation', 'A cancelled house does not hold up its payer\'s bill', 'AUTOMATED - run-all.js checks that the payer invoice resync EXCLUDES RSVP-no houses from the total, and separately that it does not zero an invoice when every house opted out. What is left is confirming a real payer with one cancelled house still gets billed correctly for the rest.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Set up two test houses sharing one phone number.\n3. Mark ONE of the two houses as RSVP \'No\' (not coming this season).\n4. Mark the OTHER house \'Done\'.\n5. Look at the menu on the left and click \'Invoices\', then the \'Nightly Automation\' tab, and press \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n6. Check that customer\'s email and invoice.', 'An invoice email should go out for the one active house, even though the other house on the same phone said no this season. The cancelled house should not block the bill and should not be included in the invoice total.', 2, '', '', 'The exclusion rule is automated. Reworded to check one real payer with a cancelled house.'],
  [191, 'Nightly Automation', 'A house needing a fix holds up its payer\'s bill', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Set up two test houses sharing one phone number, both marked \'Done\'.\n3. Flag ONE of the two houses as needing a fix (needsFix).\n4. Look at the menu on the left and click \'Invoices\', then the \'Nightly Automation\' tab, and press \'Send Invoices Now\'.\n5. Check that customer\'s email and invoice, then clear the needs-fix flag and press \'Send Invoices Now\' again.', 'No invoice email should go out while either house on that phone needs a fix, even though both are marked Done. Once the fix flag is cleared and the button is pressed again, the invoice should send with both house prices included.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [195, 'Warehouse', 'One request per member, topped up not duplicated', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse", then the tab called "Build".\n3. In "Add From Inbox", start typing a test customer\'s name in the first box and pick them from the list.\n4. Look at the Pattern box and the "How many bundles" box without typing anything into them.\n5. Click "Add to Queue".\n6. Now do steps 3 to 5 again for the SAME customer.\n7. Read the pop-up that appears, and press OK.\n8. Look at the queue below for that customer.\n9. Now pick that same customer again, change "What is this for?" to "Timer", set it to 1, and click "Add to Queue". If a warning about their record appears, read it and press OK.\n10. Look at the queue below for that customer again.\n11. Finally, pick that customer, leave it on Lights, but type a different colour into the Pattern box and click "Add to Queue".', 'At step 4 the pattern should already be filled in from their record and the bundles should already be worked out from their measured feet - you should not have to type either. At step 7 the pop-up should say they already have a request and offer to make it a bigger total. At step 8 there should be ONE heading with that customer\'s name, not two. At step 10 the timer should be a second line under that SAME heading, with the total shown in the heading. At step 11 you should get an "are you sure" pop-up first, because what you typed no longer matches their record.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [197, 'Warehouse', 'Print the build sheet as a spreadsheet', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse".\n3. Click the tab called "Build" and read what is on screen - which colour groups there are, and roughly how many houses are in each.\n4. Press the button called "Print Build Sheet" (it is just above the list).\n5. A new tab should open with the sheet in it and the print box should come up on its own. If nothing happens at all, your browser has blocked the pop-up - allow pop-ups for this site and press the button again.\n6. Instead of printing straight away, look at the preview: check the column headings across the top, and check the groups and the houses are the same ones, in the same order, as the tab you were just looking at.\n7. Pick one house you can see on both, and check its bundle number on paper matches the bundle number on screen.\n8. Print it, or save it as a PDF.\n9. Optional, if you want it in Excel: select the whole table in that tab, copy it, and paste it into a blank Excel or Google Sheets file.\n10. Go back to admin, click the tab called "Recycle", and press "Print Recycle Sheet" there.', 'The sheet must be a plain grid - one row per house or item, with headings across the top (Group, Wire, Type, Customer / Item, Address, Bin #, Feet, Bundles, Timer, Notes) and an empty tick box at the start of every row. It must list the same groups, in the same order, as the Build tab, with the group name repeated on every row rather than only in a heading. A house with no measured feet on file should leave the Feet box empty rather than printing a 0. A house that wants a timer should say YES in the Timer column. If it runs to a second page, the column headings should repeat at the top of that page. Pasting it into Excel should land in proper columns, not all in one cell. The Recycle sheet should do the same for the recycle list, with a column for the bin number that is coming back into the pool. With nothing to build, the sheet should say so rather than printing an empty grid.', 1, '', '', 'New button, 2026-08-14. The Warehouse tab is still grouped and collapsible on screen, which does not work on paper - you cannot tick a card off, and it will not open in a spreadsheet. The printed version is the same list flattened to one row per line. It is built from the same grouping code the screen uses, so the two cannot drift apart, but that is exactly what is worth checking by eye the first time.'],
  [194, 'Warehouse', 'What each house needs, on the build row', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers", then "All Customers".\n3. Click Edit on a test customer who is in the build queue. Set "Outlet Timer" to "Yes", set a wire colour, type something into their notes, and click Save.\n4. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Warehouse", then the tab called "Build".\n5. Press the "Show houses" button on that customer\'s colour group.\n6. Look at that customer\'s row.\n7. Look at the very top of the Build tab for a group called "Timers" and press its "Show houses" button.\n8. Now find a brand-new customer in the build list who has not been given a customer number yet, and look at their row.', 'That customer\'s row should show a "Timer" tag, their wire colour, their customer number, and their notes underneath. The "Timers" group at the top should list that customer by name and say how many houses in this build need a timer. The brand-new customer with no number should show an orange warning saying their number still needs assigning, instead of just showing nothing.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [193, 'Warehouse', 'Rejoining after being recycled', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Customers", then "All Customers".\n3. Click Edit on a test customer, set "RSVP" to "No", and click Save.\n4. Go to Warehouse, click the tab called "Recycle", find that customer and mark them as recycled. Write down the customer number that comes off them.\n5. Now go back to Customers, click Edit on that same customer, set "RSVP" to "Yes", and click Save.\n6. Click the tab called "Build" in Warehouse and look for that customer.\n7. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Inbox" and look in the "System" folder.\n8. Repeat the whole thing a second time, but at step 5 use the Member Portal as that customer and press Yes there instead of using admin - the answer should be identical either way.', 'After step 5 that customer should be in the Warehouse Build list, because their old lights were taken apart and a new bundle has to be made. There should be a note in the Inbox System folder saying they rejoined after recycling, that their lights need rebuilding and that they need a customer number assigned. Their record should still show NO customer number - the system must not hand one out by itself, because you may have already written a number on a bin by hand. Doing it through the Member Portal instead of admin should give exactly the same result.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [192, 'Invoices', 'Editing a customer does not resurrect a cancelled house\'s price', 'AUTOMATED - this is the same resync rule as test 190 and run-all.js covers it: a cancelled house is excluded from the payer total, so editing the customer cannot bring its price back. What is left is doing the edit once on a real record and confirming the total did not move.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Set up two test houses sharing one phone number. Mark ONE as RSVP \'No\'.\n3. Get that payer billed for the other (active) house only — for example through \'Send Invoices Now\' or by editing their invoice directly — and note the invoice total.\n4. Open \'Edit Customer\' on the active house and change something small (like a note), then save.\n5. Check that payer\'s invoice total again.', 'Saving an edit on the active house must not add the cancelled house\'s price back onto the invoice. The invoice total after step 5 should be exactly what it was before the edit — only the active house\'s price.', 2, '', '', 'Covered by the same automated resync checks as test 190. Reworded to check one real edit.'],
  [198, 'Admin', 'Refreshing the page keeps you on the same panel', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click on any panel that is NOT \'Quote Requests\' — for example click \'Customers\', then \'All Customers\'.\n3. Refresh the whole browser page (reload it).', 'After the page finishes reloading, you should land back on the same panel you were looking at (for example, \'All Customers\'), not automatically jump back to \'Quote Requests\'.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [196, 'Routes', 'Schedule tab\'s New Members panel matches real customers', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Quote Requests". Find a quote that is NOT yet Closed and note the phone number on it.\n3. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Routes and Schedule", then the "Schedule" tab (separate from the plain "Routes" tab).\n4. If a route plan is not already loaded, import a CSV that includes a house with that same phone number (or use "↺ Reload saved" if one is already imported).\n5. Click the "🆕 New Members" tab at the top of the Schedule dashboard and confirm that house is NOT listed yet.\n6. Go back to Quote Requests and either convert that quote to a customer, or set its status dropdown to "Closed" by hand.\n7. Return to the Schedule tab\'s New Members tab.', 'At step 5, before the quote is closed, that house should NOT be in the New Members list. At step 7, once the quote shows Closed, that same house should appear in the New Members list with its address and where it landed in the route plan. A house whose matching phone number has no Closed quote should never appear here, no matter how long ago it was originally added or whether its "Charge $30 new member installation fee" box happens to be ticked.', 2, '', '', 'Reworded 2026-08-14: the panel was matching the wrong signal (a fee checkbox that never clears) and showed every past customer who still had it ticked instead of just this season\'s actual signups. It now matches a Closed quote instead.'],
  [199, 'Routes', 'Print the crew\'s day as a spreadsheet', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Schedule".\n3. On the day list down the left, click the day the crew is working - normally today.\n4. Read what is on screen: how many stops that day has, and the first two or three names in order.\n5. At the top right of that day\'s panel, press the button called "Print This Day".\n6. A new tab should open with the sheet in it and the print box should come up on its own. If nothing happens at all, your browser has blocked the pop-up - allow pop-ups for this site and press the button again.\n7. Look at the preview before printing: check the column headings across the top, and check the stops are the same ones in the same order as the panel, numbered 1, 2, 3 down the page.\n8. Print it, or save it as a PDF.\n9. Click the "Fixes" tab at the top of the Schedule dashboard, pick a fixer route on the left, and press "Print This Day" there too.\n10. Click the "Takedowns" tab, pick a takedown day, and press "Print This Day" there as well.\n11. Go back to the "Scheduling" tab and press "Print Whole Plan", next to the Export CSV button.\n12. Optional, if you want it in Excel: select the whole table in that tab, copy it, and paste it into a blank Excel or Google Sheets file.', 'Each sheet must be a plain grid - one row per stop, with headings across the top (Stop, CU #, Name, Address, City, Phone, Type, Price, Details, Already done) and an empty tick box at the start of every row, so the crew can tick houses off as they go. The stops must be in the same order as the panel on screen, numbered from 1. A house with no price on the plan should leave the Price box empty rather than printing $0, and a house with no phone number should leave that box empty too. The Type column should say INSTALL, NEW or FIX so the crew can tell them apart, and anything already ticked off in the app should say "done". "Print This Day" prints the WHOLE day - both crews together. It must work the same way on all three tabs: Scheduling, Fixes and Takedowns. "Print Whole Plan" should print every day, with the date and the route repeated on every row so it can be sorted in a spreadsheet. If it runs to more than one page, the column headings should repeat at the top of each page. Pasting it into Excel should land in proper columns, not all in one cell.', 2, '', '', 'Still the same button, but it now sits next to a per-crew button for each crew (test 200 covers those). Worth one more pass to confirm "Print This Day" still prints the WHOLE day, both crews together, and did not quietly become one crew\'s sheet.'],
  [200, 'Routes', 'Two crews, a city each, and their own sheets', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Schedule".\n3. Just under the row of tabs at the top, find the box headed "Crews".\n4. There should be two crews. Type a real name over "Crew 1" - for example the names of the two people on it - then click somewhere else on the page so it saves.\n5. Do the same for "Crew 2".\n6. Refresh the whole browser page, come back to Schedule, and check both names are still there.\n7. Look at the grey text next to each crew: it should name the city that crew has, and how many stops they have on the day showing.\n8. Leave the city boxes on "Auto" for now. Click a different day on the left and watch whether the cities update to that day.\n9. Now set Crew 1\'s city box to a real town, and Crew 2\'s to a different town.\n10. On the day panel on the right, press the button with the FIRST crew\'s name on it.\n11. Look at the sheet that opens, then close it and press the button with the SECOND crew\'s name.\n12. If a yellow warning appears in the Crews box saying some stops are in neither city, press "Print This Day" as well and check those stops are on that sheet.', 'There should be exactly two crews, and both names should save when you click away and still be there after a refresh - they are stored with the route plan, so they are the same on any device you open admin on. On "Auto", the two crews should pick two DIFFERENT cities from whatever day you are looking at (the busiest one and the next busiest), and those should change as you click through days. Setting a city by hand should override Auto and stay put on every day. Each crew\'s print button must open a sheet with THAT CREW\'S NAME as the heading at the top, listing only the houses in that crew\'s city, numbered 1, 2, 3 for that crew - not the whole day filtered down with gaps in the numbering. A crew with nothing to do that day should have no button at all rather than printing an empty page. Nothing may go missing: if some stops are in neither crew\'s city, the Crews box must say so in yellow, and those stops must still appear on the "Print This Day" sheet.', 1, '', '', 'New feature, 2026-08-14. Two crews, each working one city, each printing its own sheet with its own name at the top. The names are yours to set. The city is on Auto by default because the plan does not cover the same two towns every day - pin a city to a crew if your season really does work the same two towns throughout.'],
  [201, 'Routes', 'The houses that did not get done', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click the word "Schedule".\n3. Pick a day on the left that has several houses on it, in at least two different towns, and do NOT tick anything off yet.\n4. At the top of that day\'s panel, right next to "Mark all done", there should be a reddish button reading "Not all of them got done...". Press it.\n5. A panel opens listing every house on that day with a tick box. Tick ONLY the two or three that were not finished, then press the Continue button underneath the list.\n6. The next screen should list exactly the houses you ticked, with the town each is in and where each one would go.\n7. Read the first choice: "Move them to the next day we are in that city". Check the days it names are days that already have houses in that same town.\n8. Press that first choice.\n9. Look at the day you started on, then click through to the days it said it would move them to.\n10. Now go to a different day and do it the other way round: press "Mark all done", untick the two or three that were missed, then press the button at the top which now reads "N not done - reschedule...".\n11. That should go straight to the two choices without asking you to tick anything again. This time press the SECOND choice, "Put them on another day myself".\n12. Use the "Move to..." dropdown on the first house to send it to a day of your choosing, and watch what happens to the list.\n13. Press Close, then refresh the browser page and check the moves stuck.', '"Mark all done" must still work exactly as before - it is a fast start, not something that got replaced. The reschedule button must be sitting next to it on ANY day that still has something outstanding, including a day nobody has touched yet; it should only disappear once every house on that day is finished. On an untouched day it opens on the "which ones did not get done" tick list, where a tick means the house was NOT finished - the opposite of the tick boxes on the day itself - and everything you leave unticked gets marked done when you press Continue. On a day you have already ticked through it must SKIP that step and go straight to the two choices, trusting the ticks you made; a "Wrong list? Choose again" button gets you back to the tick list. The list of misses must contain only the houses you ticked. For each one it should name the next later day that ALREADY has houses in that same town - not simply the next day on the calendar. After pressing the first choice, those houses should be gone from the day you started on and sitting on the days it named, still unticked - they were not done, so they must not arrive marked as done. Any house in a town that no later day goes to must be called out in yellow and LEFT WHERE IT IS. It must never be quietly dumped on some other day. The second choice should let you pick a day per house, one at a time; each house should drop off the list as you move it, the screen should stay on the day you are working through rather than jumping to the day you just moved a house to, and the panel should close itself once the list is empty. Everything must survive a refresh - the plan saves itself.', 2, '', '', 'Changed 2026-08-15. The reschedule button used to stay hidden until at least one house had been ticked off, which meant the whole feature was invisible unless you happened to press Mark all done first. It now sits next to Mark all done on any day with something still outstanding, and on an untouched day it opens a tick list where ticking a house means it was NOT finished.'],
  [202, 'Routes', 'A new customer lands on the next day in their city', 'AUTOMATED - the dates are checked on every commit (run-all.js suite 17): that a November house is never given an October date, that the earliest matching day in the right city wins, and that a fix or removal day does not count as being in that town. What is left for a person is that it actually happens on screen, with real routes.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Routes and Schedule".\n3. Make sure there is at least one saved install route in the calendar for some city - say Lehi - on a date in the future. Write down the date and how many stops it has.\n4. In the menu on the left, click "Customers", then the tab "Add a Customer".\n5. Add a test customer at an address in that same city. Leave Installation Timing Preference on "Any".\n6. Read the message next to the Add Customer button after it saves.\n7. Go back to "Routes and Schedule" and open that saved route.\n8. Now add a SECOND test customer, same city, but this time set Installation Timing Preference to "November".\n9. Read the message next to the Add Customer button again.', 'Step 6: the message should say they were put on the route for the date you wrote down in step 3, in that city.\n\nStep 7: the first test customer should now be on that route. Nobody should be bumped off that day unless adding your test customer takes it OVER 40 houses - a day of twelve must stay a day of twelve, and nobody\'s confirmed date should move for no reason. If it DOES go over 40, one of last year\'s houses (never a new hang, never the customer you just added) should have moved to the next day you are in that city, with a note under Customer Messages in the "System" tab naming who moved and to when.\n\nStep 9: if it is still October or earlier, the November customer should NOT be put on that October day. They should either go on your first November day in that city, or the message should say there is no day booked in that city on or after November 1st. They must never be given an October date.', 2, '', '', 'Changed 2026-08-15. The bump rule changed: a day used to shed a house every single time a customer was added, to hold whatever size it was planned at. Now it only sheds one when the day goes over 40, and the house that goes is one of last year\'s rather than whoever happened to be last.'],
  [203, 'Routes', 'The upcoming routes keep themselves accurate', 'AUTOMATED - run-all.js suite 18 runs the whole sweep against a fake database on every commit: past days are never edited, fix and removal days are left alone, a cancelled or deleted house comes off, a house booked on two days keeps the earlier one, and a set of routes that is already right writes nothing at all. What is left for a person is watching it happen with real data.\n\nThe sweep runs by itself about 20 seconds after you sign in, and every 15 minutes after that. You never press anything.\n\n1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left, click \'Routes and Schedule\' and find a saved install route on a FUTURE date. Write down which houses are on it.\n3. In the menu on the left, click \'Customers\', then the tab \'All Customers\'.\n4. Find one of the houses you wrote down and click Edit.\n5. Change their street address to something different, and save.\n6. Find a DIFFERENT house from that same route, click Edit, and set their RSVP to No. Save.\n7. Wait up to 15 minutes - or sign out, sign back in, and wait about 20 seconds.\n8. Go back to Routes and Schedule and open that same route again.\n9. In the menu on the left, click \'Customer Messages\', then the \'System\' tab.', 'Back on the route in step 8: the first house should now show the NEW address you typed, not the old one. The house you set to No should be gone from the route completely.\n\nIn System notices there should be ONE new note - not one per house - called \'Routes Kept Up To Date\', naming what changed: the stop that was refreshed and the house that came off. It should also remind you that nobody has told the customer about any date that moved.\n\nNothing on any PAST route should have changed at all - those are the record of what the crew was actually sent out with.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [204, 'Admin', 'Health Check catches two customers on one phone number', '1. Open the Admin Dashboard and go to the Health Check tab.\n2. Look for the row titled \'Two different customers share one phone number\'.\n3. If it says everything is fine, make a test case: open All Customers, pick any two customers with DIFFERENT names who are not related, and edit the second one so their phone number is exactly the same as the first one. Save.\n4. Go back to Health Check and let it re-run (it runs on its own when you open the tab).\n5. When you are finished testing, put the second customer\'s real phone number back.', 'After step 4 the row should list the two customers together, showing both names, the shared number, and both addresses.\n\nWhy this matters: the invoice is filed under the phone number, so those two customers would have become ONE bill. One of them gets billed for both houses and the other is never billed at all - and neither is billed until BOTH houses are installed, so one unfinished house can stop the other being billed for the whole season.\n\nThere is deliberately no Fix button. Only you can tell whether it is two households that need separate numbers, or one payer whose second house should have Bill To set. A couple who genuinely want one bill is NOT flagged, and neither is one person with two houses under the same name - if either of those shows up here, that is a bug.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [205, 'Admin', 'Quotes - convert to customer automatically', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Quote Requests".\n3. Find an approved quote that has everything on it - a name, address, phone, email, a photo, an approved price and light colours. Write down the name, the price, the light colours, and whether "Charge $30 one-time set-up fee" is ticked on that quote.\n4. Click "Convert to Customer".\n5. In the popup, click "Convert automatically". You should stay on the Quote Requests screen - it should NOT take you to the Add a Customer form.\n6. Watch for the little dark message that slides up at the bottom of the screen, and write down exactly what it says.\n7. Click "Customers" in the left menu, then "All Customers", and find the person you just converted.\n8. Open them and check their price, their light colours, and their Number of Bins.\n9. Click "Invoices" in the left menu and find their invoice.\n10. Click "Warehouse" in the left menu and look at the build queue for their light colours.\n11. Go back to "Quote Requests".', 'You should never have left the Quote Requests screen. The message at the bottom should name the customer, give their new customer number and bin count, say they are in the Warehouse, and - if the $30 fee was ticked on the quote - say the $30 set-up fee was applied.\n\nThe customer record should exist with the approved price and the light colours from the quote. An invoice should exist for them. They should be listed in the Warehouse build queue under their colours. Back in Quote Requests, that quote should now say Converted, and the Convert to Customer button should be gone from it.\n\nThe whole point: automatic does exactly the same job as filling the form in by hand, without you having to look at the form. If ANY of those things is missing - no invoice, not in the warehouse, no customer number, wrong price, or the $30 fee ticked on the quote but not charged - that is a fail, and say which one.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [206, 'Admin', 'Converting a thin quote warns you first', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard) and click "Quote Requests".\n2. Find (or make) a quote that is missing things - no photo, or no email, or no price - but that DOES have light colours on it.\n3. Click "Convert to Customer".\n4. Read the popup carefully before clicking anything.\n5. Click "Cancel" and check the popup goes away and nothing was created.\n6. Click "Convert to Customer" again and this time click "Convert automatically".\n7. Read the message that slides up at the bottom of the screen.\n8. Now find (or make) a quote with NO light colours at all, and click "Convert to Customer" on that one.\n9. Look at the popup - do not click anything yet.', 'In step 4 the popup should show a red box listing exactly what that quote is missing, by name - for example "The quote has no House Picture, Total Price." A quote that has everything should NOT show that red box at all.\n\nIn step 5, Cancel should close the popup and create nothing - check All Customers to be sure they were not added.\n\nIn step 7, the message should still say they were converted, AND it should end by saying what is still missing. Converting automatically deliberately does not stop to ask you about missing details - it saves them with what the quote has - but it must always TELL you what it went ahead without. If it converts silently with no mention of what is missing, that is a fail.\n\nSteps 8 and 9 are the exception to all of the above. Light colours are the ONE thing a customer cannot be saved without, because with no colours nothing goes to the Warehouse to be built. So on a quote with no colours the popup should show a red warning saying so, and the "Convert automatically" button should be greyed out and unclickable, reading "Not available - this quote has no light colours." Only "Fill in manually" should work, and it opens the form with the colour picker on it. If that button is still clickable on a colourless quote, that is a fail.', 2, '', '', 'Light colours became a required field on 2026-08-15, so the automatic path can no longer save a quote that has none. Steps 8 and 9 are new: the popup greys out "Convert automatically" and says why, instead of letting you click it and get an error back.'],
  [207, 'Routes', 'Forty houses a day, filled both ways', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Routes and Schedule".\n3. Find a saved install route for some city - say Lehi - on a future date, with well under 40 stops on it. Write down the date and the count.\n4. Make sure there is a SECOND saved install route for that same city on a LATER date, with houses on it. Write down its count too.\n5. Find a customer in that city who is NOT scheduled and has the "Charge $30 new member installation fee" box ticked - that box is what makes them a New Hang. Note their name, and check the Routes list shows them with the pink "New Hang - Install ASAP" badge.\n6. Wait for the automatic sweep, or sign out and back in and wait about twenty seconds. Then look at both routes again.\n7. Look at Customer Messages, "System" tab, for a note titled "Routes Kept Up To Date".\n8. Check the size of every upcoming day.\n9. Now find a customer whose Installation Timing Preference is "November", who is not scheduled, in a city where you have an OCTOBER route day that is not full. Wait for the next sweep and check where they landed.\n10. Find a day that is over 40 in a city where there is NO later day booked, and read the System notice.', 'Step 6: the first day should have FILLED UP to 40 - not stayed at the count you wrote down. 40 is a target, not just a ceiling; we want as many people in every day as possible. It fills from two places, in this order: first customers who are on no day at all, then by pulling houses FORWARD off the later day in that same city. So the later day should have shrunk by however many were needed. The New Hang from step 5 should be on a day now, and the pink badge should be gone. They get a seat before anybody who already had a date. Step 7: the note should say how many were put on a day and how many were brought forward, naming the first few. It should NOT list hundreds of names one per line. If it says there is more to do, that is normal on the first run - one sweep only takes on so much at a time and the rest follows about fifteen minutes later. Step 8: no upcoming day should be over 40, except a day in a city with no later day booked - see step 10. Step 9: THE IMPORTANT ONE. The November customer must NEVER be pulled onto an October day. Filling a day forward is the only place in the whole rescheduler that can break somebody\'s timing preference, so it checks every single house before moving it earlier. They should either stay where they are or go on a November day in their city. Step 10: a day IS allowed to sit over 40 when there is no later day in that city, and the notice must say so in as many words - naming the date, the city, how many it has, and telling you to build another day for that city. A New Hang is never left unscheduled just to hold the cap. Nothing anywhere should move a house to an earlier day it is not allowed on, lose a house, or put one on two days at once.', 2, '', '', 'Changed 2026-08-15, same day it was written. 40 was a ceiling only - a quiet day was left quiet. Addie: "a day of 12 should fill up to 40, we want as many people in every day as possible." Days now fill from the unscheduled pool and by pulling houses forward off later days, which is why step 9 (a November customer must never be dragged onto an October day) is the one to actually test.'],
  [208, 'Admin', 'All Customers shows the next day the crew is coming', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Look at the menu on the left side of the screen and click "Customers", then "All Customers".\n3. Look at the "Route" column - the third one across, holding the difficulty badge (Easy/Medium/Hard) and the word Scheduled or Unscheduled. Underneath those, EVERY row should now carry a small rounded label. If a day is booked it names it; if not it says "No day booked yet".\n4. Try to click one of those labels.\n5. Find a customer who is scheduled for an install but has NOT been marked complete. Check what their label says.\n6. Find a customer whose install IS marked complete and who has a takedown booked. Check what their label says.\n7. Find a customer who is not scheduled for anything at all.\n8. Find a customer whose scheduled day has already passed and who was never ticked off as done.\n9. Open that same customer in Routes and confirm the date shown in All Customers is the same date the route says.', 'Step 3: the label should read the type of visit and the day - for example "Hang Wed, Nov 12" or "Takedown Tue, Jan 6". It is the SOONER of the two: whichever the crew is coming for next. Step 4: nothing should happen. It is a label, not a button - there should be no hover effect and no click. Step 5: it should say Hang and the install date. Step 6: it should say Takedown and the removal date, NOT the install date. Once the lights are up the install is not coming up any more, so it must stop being the answer. Step 7: they should show a greyed-out label with a dashed outline reading "No day booked yet". It must NOT be blank. Out of season nobody is scheduled at all, so a blank here would mean the whole column looks empty and you cannot tell the feature is working from one that is broken. Step 8: the label should still be there, but in amber with a warning triangle. That is a house that got missed, and it is exactly the row somebody needs to spot - it must not be hidden just because the date has passed. Step 9: the two dates must match. If they do not, the routes and the customer record disagree and that is worth reporting.', 2, '', '', 'Changed 2026-08-15, hours after it shipped. The label used to be left off entirely when nobody was booked. Out of season that is EVERY customer, so the column drew blank everywhere and the feature could not be found at all. It now always draws something, and step 7 is the one to check.'],
  [209, 'Warehouse', 'Light colours written as words, not picked', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. In the menu on the left side of the screen, click "Health Check".\n3. Press "Run Health Check".\n4. Find the row titled "Customer whose light colours are written as words".\n5. Write down how many customers it lists. Read a few of them - each row shows what that customer\'s colours currently say, and which part of it the app could not read.\n6. Pick one of them. Click "Customers" in the left menu, then "All Customers", find that customer and click Edit.\n7. In the light colour picker, tick the colours that match what their description was trying to say. If you cannot tell what it meant, leave them alone and pick a different one - do NOT guess.\n8. Save.\n9. Go back to Health Check and press "Run Health Check" again.\n10. Click "Warehouse" in the left menu and look at the Build tab.', 'That customer should be GONE from the Health Check list and the count should have dropped by one. In the Warehouse Build tab they should now be sitting in the normal group for those colours, with their bundles counted in with everybody else, instead of alone in a group of their own.\n\nThere is deliberately no Fix button on this row. The app will not guess what the words meant, because getting it wrong changes what the crew physically builds - only a person can decide what "red with tinsel" was supposed to be.\n\nOne thing to watch: this list should only ever SHRINK. Nothing in the app types words into a light description any more - every way in is the colour picker - so everything on this list is old data. If a customer you added RECENTLY turns up here, that is a bug: something has started writing free text again, and it needs reporting.', 1, '', '', ''],
  [210, 'Routes', 'Everyone gets routed unless they are Maybe Next Year', '1. Log into admin (the private office dashboard).\n2. Look at the menu on the left and click "Customers", then "All Customers".\n3. Pick any ordinary customer who is NOT marked Maybe Next Year. Look at the Route column - most people will read RSVP: Pending, because the RSVP email has never gone out.\n4. Now go to "Routes and Schedule" and open the route builder.\n5. Choose that customer\'s city, set the counts, pick a date and press "Generate Install Route".\n6. Check whether Pending customers appear among the stops.\n7. Save the route.\n8. Go back to All Customers and look at that customer\'s Route column again.\n9. Now mark somebody Maybe Next Year, then generate another route for their city.\n10. Mark somebody else\'s RSVP as No, and generate again.\n11. Wait for the automatic sweep (or sign out and in and wait twenty seconds), then read the note under Customer Messages, "System" tab.', 'Step 6: customers whose RSVP is Pending or blank MUST appear. This is the fix - the generator used to require an RSVP of exactly "Yes", and the only thing in the whole app that ever sets that is converting a quote to a customer, so all ~945 imported houses were silently excluded and routes came back empty or tiny. Step 8: their label should now name the day they are on, and the Route column should say Scheduled. Step 9: a Maybe Next Year customer must NEVER appear on a generated route. That is the one label that keeps somebody out. Step 10: an RSVP of No must also stay off. They told us not to come, and the nightly sweep strips them off routes anyway - if they appeared, the two would fight each other every fifteen minutes. Step 11: the note should end with a line saying who is still waiting for a day, counted per town, with roughly how many more days each town needs - and it should tell you to build those days in Routes. Anyone with no map pin on their address should be called out separately, because no number of extra days will ever fit them. Nothing here should schedule somebody twice, and nobody who is already installed should be picked up again.', 1, '', '', ''],
];
